-- 2) Hotspots per city.
values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tower-of-london$dyk$, $dyk$Tower of London$dyk$, $dyk$The fortress guarding London for 950 years$dyk$, $dyk$Rising before you is the Tower of London – a fortress that has witnessed nearly a thousand years of England's most dramatic history. William the Conqueror began the White Tower, the great keep at its heart, around 1078 to show the conquered city of London exactly who was in charge. Since then, the Tower has served as royal palace, prison, mint, armoury and even a zoo housing lions and a polar bear. But it is as a prison and place of execution that it became infamous. Elizabeth I was held captive here as a young princess, and her mother Anne Boleyn was executed here in 1536 on the orders of Henry VIII. Today the Tower is guarded by the Yeoman Warders, better known as Beefeaters, and the British Crown Jewels are kept here behind armoured glass. Every evening for over 700 years, the gates have been locked in the venerable Ceremony of the Keys. And then there are the ravens – but that's a story all of its own. Welcome to England's bloodiest and most fascinating building.$dyk$, 51.5081, -0.0759, 80, $dyk$1078$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tower-bridge$dyk$, $dyk$Tower Bridge$dyk$, $dyk$The queen of bridges over the Thames$dyk$, $dyk$Tower Bridge is probably the most famous bridge in the world – and the one most tourists insist on calling London Bridge. It opened in 1894 after eight years of construction and was a technical marvel: a bascule bridge whose two 1,000-ton halves could be raised in just minutes using steam-driven hydraulics, allowing tall ships to reach the docks upstream. The neo-Gothic towers are actually steel frames clad in stone, designed to harmonise with their neighbour, the Tower of London. The bascules are still raised hundreds of times a year, now powered by electricity, and river traffic always takes priority over road traffic – even presidents have had to wait. Up in the walkways between the towers, 42 metres above the Thames, you can now walk across a glass floor and watch the traffic pass beneath your feet. The walkways were originally built so pedestrians could cross even when the bridge was open, but they quickly became a haunt for pickpockets and were closed in 1910. Today they are the bridge's biggest attraction.$dyk$, 51.5055, -0.0754, 70, $dyk$1894$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$big-ben$dyk$, $dyk$Big Ben$dyk$, $dyk$The most famous clock in the world$dyk$, $dyk$You are standing before the most photographed clock tower in the world – and here's a secret: Big Ben isn't actually the tower. Big Ben is the name of the 13.7-ton Great Bell hanging inside it. The tower itself was long known simply as the Clock Tower, but was renamed Elizabeth Tower in 2012 to honour Queen Elizabeth II's sixty years on the throne. The tower was completed in 1859 as part of the new Houses of Parliament, after the old palace burned down in 1834. Within months, the Great Bell cracked – a hammer that was too heavy had been used – and it is in fact that cracked bell you still hear today, rotated so the hammer strikes an undamaged spot. That's what gives the chime its utterly distinctive tone. The clock mechanism is famed for its precision: when it needs adjusting, old penny coins are placed on the pendulum – one coin changes the speed by two-fifths of a second per day. Big Ben's chimes are broadcast live on BBC radio, a tradition since 1924, and for Britons these notes are the very sound of the nation.$dyk$, 51.50072, -0.12462, 50, $dyk$1859$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$st-pauls-cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$St Paul's Cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Wren's masterpiece that defied the bombs$dyk$, $dyk$When the Great Fire of 1666 reduced medieval London to ashes, the architect Christopher Wren was given the chance to create something entirely new. The result, completed in 1710, is the cathedral before you – crowned by one of the largest domes in the world, 111 metres high, which dominated London's skyline for over 250 years. No building was allowed to rise higher than St Paul's until 1963. Inside the dome is the famous Whispering Gallery, where a whisper against the wall can be heard 30 metres away on the opposite side – an acoustic quirk that has baffled visitors for centuries. The cathedral's most iconic moment came during the Blitz in December 1940, when the photograph of the dome rising unscathed from smoke and flames became a symbol of British resilience. Churchill ordered that the cathedral be saved at all costs, and volunteer fire watchers slept on the roof. Churchill's state funeral was held here in 1965, and Prince Charles married Diana here in 1981. In the crypt rest Nelson, Wellington – and Wren himself, beneath the words: 'Reader, if you seek his monument – look around you.'$dyk$, 51.5138, -0.0984, 70, $dyk$1710$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$rosetta-stone-british-museum$dyk$, $dyk$The Rosetta Stone, British Museum$dyk$, $dyk$The stone that cracked the hieroglyphic code$dyk$, $dyk$Inside the British Museum, just steps from the entrance, stands the museum's most visited object: the Rosetta Stone. It looks unremarkable – a grey, chipped slab of rock – but this is the key that unlocked an entire civilisation. The stone was carved in 196 BC and bears a priestly decree honouring the young pharaoh Ptolemy V, written three times: in Egyptian hieroglyphs, in Egyptian Demotic script, and in classical Greek. When French soldiers found it in 1799 near the town of Rashid – Rosetta – in the Nile Delta, scholars immediately grasped its value: since the Greek could be read, there was finally a way to decipher the hieroglyphs, which had been a mystery for 1,400 years. The race was won in 1822 by the Frenchman Jean-François Champollion, who cracked the code and in doing so founded Egyptology as a science. The stone passed into British hands after Napoleon's defeat in Egypt and has been displayed at the British Museum since 1802. Egypt has repeatedly requested its return – the debate over where it truly belongs continues to this day.$dyk$, 51.5194, -0.127, 70, $dyk$196 BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$nelsons-column$dyk$, $dyk$Nelson's Column, Trafalgar Square$dyk$, $dyk$The naval hero watching over London$dyk$, $dyk$Atop the 52-metre granite column before you stands Admiral Horatio Nelson – the man who saved Britain from Napoleon's invasion plans but paid with his life. At the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805, Nelson's fleet crushed the combined Franco-Spanish force without losing a single ship. Nelson himself was struck by a French sharpshooter's bullet in the midst of the fighting and died aboard his flagship HMS Victory, hours after victory was secured. His famous final signal to the fleet – 'England expects that every man will do his duty' – can still be quoted by Britons from memory. The column was erected in 1843, and the statue on top is over five metres tall, even if it looks tiny from down here. The four bronze lions at the base, sculpted by the artist Edwin Landseer, arrived only in 1867 – twenty-four years late. Landseer was a painter of animals, not a sculptor, and is said to have worked from a dead lion from the zoo that began to decompose before he finished. Look closely: the lions' backs are concave like a dog's, which real lions' backs are not. Nelson gazes south-west – towards Trafalgar and the sea.$dyk$, 51.50775, -0.12797, 60, $dyk$1843$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$st-jamess-park$dyk$, $dyk$St James's Park$dyk$, $dyk$A royal oasis with pelicans$dyk$, $dyk$You are standing in London's oldest royal park – a green lung nestled between three palaces: Buckingham Palace, St James's Palace and Westminster. Henry VIII bought the land in 1532; back then it was marshy ground where pigs grazed. He drained it and turned it into a royal deer park. James I then kept an exotic menagerie here in the early 1600s, complete with camels and crocodiles, and Charles II opened the park to the public, strolling here himself with his mistresses and his spaniels. Today's romantic landscape with its winding lake was created in the 1820s by the architect John Nash, the same man who shaped Regent Street and the façade of Buckingham Palace. The park's most beloved residents are the pelicans – the first arrived in 1664 as a gift from the Russian ambassador, and their successors have lived by the lake ever since. Make your way to the bridge over the lake: the view eastward towards the rooftops and turrets of Horse Guards is often called one of the loveliest in London. This is the perfect place to simply sink into a deckchair and breathe.$dyk$, 51.5027, -0.1348, 80, $dyk$1532$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tate-modern$dyk$, $dyk$Tate Modern$dyk$, $dyk$The power station turned temple of art$dyk$, $dyk$The mighty brick colossus before you was once Bankside Power Station – an oil-fired plant that supplied London with electricity. The building was designed by Giles Gilbert Scott, the same architect who created Britain's iconic red telephone box, and the power station closed in 1981. Instead of demolition, salvation came from art: after a spectacular conversion by Swiss architects Herzog & de Meuron, Tate Modern opened in 2000 and almost instantly became one of the most visited modern art museums in the world. Its heart is the dizzying Turbine Hall – 35 metres high and 152 metres long, where the turbines once stood. It now hosts colossal art installations that have become world-famous, such as Olafur Eliasson's artificial sun in 2003, which drew millions of visitors who lay down on the floor to bask in its glow. The collections span Picasso, Dalí, Warhol, Rothko and contemporary art from across the globe – and the main displays are free to visit. Don't miss the viewing terrace in the Blavatnik Building, the 2016 extension, with panoramic views over the Thames and the dome of St Paul's directly across the river.$dyk$, 51.5076, -0.0994, 70, $dyk$2000$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$covent-garden$dyk$, $dyk$Covent Garden$dyk$, $dyk$From vegetable market to street theatre$dyk$, $dyk$The square you're standing on has been London's living room for nearly 400 years. The name comes from 'convent garden' – this was once the kitchen garden of Westminster Abbey. In the 1630s the architect Inigo Jones designed England's first classical piazza here, inspired by Italian squares, and from 1654 a fruit and vegetable market grew up that would become London's largest. The handsome market building at its centre was built in 1830. For nearly three centuries this was a bustle of traders, flower girls and porters – the setting where George Bernard Shaw had the flower girl Eliza Doolittle meet Professor Higgins in his play Pygmalion, better known as the musical My Fair Lady. In 1974 the wholesale market moved out, and the square transformed into today's blend of shops, cafés and, above all, street performers. Covent Garden has a special place in theatre history: the Royal Opera House stands here, and as early as 1662 the diarist Samuel Pepys watched England's first recorded Punch and Judy puppet show on this very piazza. The performers you see today have passed genuine auditions – and the quality shows.$dyk$, 51.5117, -0.1226, 70, $dyk$1830$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$abbey-road-crossing$dyk$, $dyk$The Abbey Road Crossing$dyk$, $dyk$The most famous zebra stripes on Earth$dyk$, $dyk$An ordinary zebra crossing on a quiet residential street – and yet tourists from all over the world stand here waiting for a gap in the traffic to take exactly the same photo. At half past eleven on the morning of 8 August 1969, John, Ringo, Paul and George stepped out of the EMI studio behind you and walked across the road while photographer Iain Macmillan stood on a stepladder in the middle of the street. A policeman held up the traffic. The whole shoot took ten minutes and produced six frames – the fifth became the cover of Abbey Road, the last album the Beatles ever recorded. Paul walking barefoot was pure chance – it was a hot day and he kicked off his sandals – but the detail fuelled the bizarre 'Paul is dead' conspiracy theory, in which the cover was said to depict a funeral procession. The studio behind the wall, Abbey Road Studios, opened back in 1931, inaugurated by the composer Edward Elgar, and is still going strong – everything from Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon to the scores for Star Wars and The Lord of the Rings has been recorded here. In 2010 the crossing was granted heritage status – one of very few road crossings in the world with that protection.$dyk$, 51.53216, -0.17739, 50, $dyk$1969$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$platform-nine-and-three-quarters$dyk$, $dyk$Platform 9¾, King's Cross$dyk$, $dyk$Where the journey to Hogwarts begins$dyk$, $dyk$Inside King's Cross station is a place that shouldn't exist: Platform 9¾. This is where J.K. Rowling had Harry Potter and every Hogwarts student board the Hogwarts Express, by running straight at the brick wall between platforms 9 and 10. Since the books became a global phenomenon after their 1997 debut, the station has turned into a pilgrimage site, and the station's management has embraced it with a wink: in the concourse hangs a 'Platform 9¾' sign, and beneath it a luggage trolley that appears to be passing through the wall – half of it has already 'vanished' inside. The queue to be photographed wearing a scarf in your Hogwarts house colours can be long, and there's a Harry Potter shop right next door. The funny part is that Rowling herself has admitted to a mistake: when she wrote the scene she was picturing the platforms at Euston station, not King's Cross – at the real King's Cross, platforms 9 and 10 are separated by tracks, not a wall. The films solved this by shooting at platforms 4 and 5. The station itself, incidentally, is a Victorian masterpiece dating from 1852.$dyk$, 51.53195, -0.124, 60, $dyk$1997$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$trafalgar-square-police-box$dyk$, $dyk$Britain's Smallest Police Station$dyk$, $dyk$The police station inside a lamp post$dyk$, $dyk$In the south-east corner of Trafalgar Square stands what looks like an unusually fat granite lamp post. Look closer: it has a door, narrow window slits – and is often called Britain's smallest police station. The story begins in the 1920s, when Trafalgar Square was London's natural stage for demonstrations and protests. The police wanted a lookout post at the centre of the action, but the public objected to plans for a police box that would spoil the famous square. The solution in 1926 was ingeniously discreet: one of the square's massive granite pillars was simply hollowed out and fitted with an interior. Inside there was room for a single constable, with a telephone line direct to headquarters. When the receiver was lifted, the lamp on top began to flash, alerting nearby officers that backup was needed. Through the observation slits, the constable could discreetly keep watch over the entire square. The space is said to have held two prisoners in an emergency – though extremely snugly. Today the police have abandoned their smallest station, and the historic lookout has been given a rather more prosaic assignment: it's used as a storage cupboard by Westminster's cleaning staff.$dyk$, 51.50737, -0.12706, 40, $dyk$1926$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$london-stone$dyk$, $dyk$London Stone$dyk$, $dyk$The stone that protects London$dyk$, $dyk$Behind a small grille in the façade of 111 Cannon Street sits an unremarkable lump of limestone, barely half a metre wide. Most people walk past without realising this is London Stone – perhaps the most myth-laden object in the city. The stone is mentioned in writing as early as around 1100, when it was already a famous landmark; in the Middle Ages, addresses were given in relation to it. What it originally was, nobody knows: theories range from a Roman milestone to a druidic altar or a magical guardian stone. Legend has it that the stone was brought here by Brutus of Troy, London's mythical founder, and that 'so long as the Stone of Brutus is safe, so long shall London flourish'. If the stone is destroyed, the city falls. In 1450 the rebel leader Jack Cade rode into London, struck the stone with his sword and declared himself lord of the city – a scene Shakespeare immortalised in Henry VI. The stone has survived both the Great Fire of 1666 and the bombs of the Blitz, and when it was temporarily moved to the Museum of London in 2016, the press muttered that the city was in peril. Since 2018 it has been back in its rightful place. London still stands – for now.$dyk$, 51.51151, -0.08955, 40, $dyk$c. 1100$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$fifty-berkeley-square$dyk$, $dyk$50 Berkeley Square$dyk$, $dyk$The most haunted house in London$dyk$, $dyk$The elegant 18th-century townhouse before you looks harmless enough – but in Victorian times, hardly anyone dared approach number 50 Berkeley Square. The house was built around 1750 and was once home to Prime Minister George Canning. But it is the attic room that made the address infamous as 'the most haunted house in London'. According to legend, the room is haunted by a young woman who threw herself from the top floor to escape a cruel relative; her spirit is said to appear as a brown mist – and anyone who sees it is frightened into madness or death. The stories flourished throughout the 19th century: a nobleman who wagered he could spend a night in the room was found paralysed with terror, and in the most famous version, from 1887, two sailors broke in for the night – one fled, and the other was found dead, impaled on the railings outside. Sceptics point out that the house's eccentric owner Thomas Myers, who lived here alone and increasingly unkempt in the 1860s, probably gave rise to the rumours of figures at the windows. Since the 1930s the antiquarian booksellers Maggs Bros have occupied the building – and the staff reported, officially at least, no ghosts.$dyk$, 51.50986, -0.1465, 40, $dyk$1750$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$London$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sweeney-todd-fleet-street$dyk$, $dyk$Sweeney Todd's Barber Shop$dyk$, $dyk$The demon barber of Fleet Street$dyk$, $dyk$186 Fleet Street, right next to the church of St Dunstan-in-the-West – this is where London's bloodiest legend is said to have unfolded. According to the tale, the barber Sweeney Todd ran his shop at this very address. Customers were seated in a specially built chair positioned over a trapdoor; with a flick of a lever, the chair tipped backwards and the victim plunged into the cellar, where Todd cut their throats with his razor. The bodies – over a hundred, according to legend – were then turned into the filling of the meat pies sold by his accomplice Mrs Lovett, whose pie shop stood a short distance away on Bell Yard. The customers ate in blissful ignorance, and the pies are said to have been extremely popular. But did Sweeney Todd really exist? Almost certainly not. The story first appeared in 1846–47 as the serial 'The String of Pearls' in a penny dreadful, the cheap horror literature of the Victorian era, and no court records or contemporary sources support the existence of any demon barber. Yet the legend refuses to die: it has become stage plays, Stephen Sondheim's acclaimed 1979 musical and Tim Burton's 2007 film. So feel free to get a haircut in London – just maybe not here.$dyk$, 51.51422, -0.10893, 40, $dyk$1846$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$eiffel-tower$dyk$, $dyk$Eiffel Tower$dyk$, $dyk$The iron lady of Paris$dyk$, $dyk$Look up – 330 metres of iron lattice rise above you, the most visited paid monument in the world. When Gustave Eiffel's tower was unveiled for the 1889 World's Fair, marking the centenary of the French Revolution, many Parisians were horrified. Three hundred artists and writers signed a furious protest against this 'useless and monstrous' tower, and the novelist Guy de Maupassant reportedly ate lunch in its restaurant regularly – because it was the only place in Paris where he didn't have to look at it. The tower was meant to stand for just twenty years, then be dismantled. What saved it was science: Eiffel installed a weather station and, crucially, a radio antenna at the top, making the tower too useful to tear down. During the First World War, its transmitters intercepted enemy messages. It took 18,038 iron pieces and 2.5 million rivets to build, and every seven years around 60 tonnes of paint keep the iron lady from rusting. She even changes height: on hot summer days, thermal expansion makes the tower grow by up to 15 centimetres.$dyk$, 48.85837, 2.29448, 80, $dyk$1889$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$notre-dame-cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Notre-Dame de Paris$dyk$, $dyk$The heart of France, reborn from fire$dyk$, $dyk$You are standing before the cathedral that is, quite literally, the heart of France – all road distances in the country are measured from a marker just outside its doors. Construction of Notre-Dame began in 1163 on the Île de la Cité, the island where Paris was born, and took nearly two hundred years to complete. This Gothic masterpiece pioneered the use of flying buttresses, those elegant stone arms bracing the walls, allowing them to soar higher and hold vast windows of stained glass. Here Napoleon crowned himself Emperor in 1804, famously taking the crown from the Pope's hands and placing it on his own head. By the 1830s the cathedral was so neglected that demolition was discussed – it was Victor Hugo's novel The Hunchback of Notre-Dame, written partly to save the building, that rekindled the nation's love for it. The world watched in horror in April 2019 as fire destroyed the roof and toppled the spire. After a five-year restoration involving some 2,000 craftspeople, Notre-Dame reopened in December 2024, its limestone glowing brighter than it has in centuries.$dyk$, 48.85297, 2.35003, 70, $dyk$1163$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$mona-lisa-louvre$dyk$, $dyk$The Mona Lisa, Louvre$dyk$, $dyk$The most famous smile in the world$dyk$, $dyk$Inside the world's largest museum hangs a surprisingly small poplar panel, just 77 by 53 centimetres, protected by bulletproof glass and admired by some 20,000 people every day. Leonardo da Vinci began painting the Mona Lisa around 1503, probably portraying Lisa Gherardini, wife of a Florentine silk merchant – hence her Italian name, La Gioconda. Leonardo never considered it finished; he carried it with him for years, refining it, and brought it to France when King Francis I invited him there. That is why Italy's most famous portrait belongs to France. Her fame, though, owes much to a crime. In 1911 an Italian handyman named Vincenzo Peruggia simply lifted the painting off the wall, hid it under his smock and walked out. For two years the empty wall drew bigger crowds than the painting ever had, and newspapers worldwide printed her face. When Peruggia was caught trying to sell her in Florence, the Mona Lisa returned as the most famous artwork on Earth. Look at her eyes as you move – they really do seem to follow you, an effect of Leonardo's masterful soft shading.$dyk$, 48.86061, 2.33641, 70, $dyk$1503$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$arc-de-triomphe$dyk$, $dyk$Arc de Triomphe$dyk$, $dyk$Napoleon's arch of victories$dyk$, $dyk$Twelve grand avenues radiate out from the monument before you like the points of a star – which is why this square was long called Place de l'Étoile. Napoleon ordered the arch in 1806, the day after his greatest victory at Austerlitz, promising his soldiers they would 'return home through arches of triumph'. He never saw it finished: the 50-metre colossus took thirty years to complete, and Napoleon only passed beneath it in 1840 – as a coffin, when his remains were brought home from Saint Helena. The names of 660 generals and 158 battles are engraved on its walls; the underlined names are those who fell in combat. Beneath the arch lies the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier from the First World War, and its eternal flame is rekindled every evening at 6.30 – a ceremony that has never been missed since 1923, even during the Nazi occupation. In 1919, the aviator Charles Godefroy flew a biplane straight through the arch's central vault to honour the airmen of the war. Climb the 284 steps to the roof and you'll get the finest view in Paris – with the Champs-Élysées rolling out at your feet.$dyk$, 48.8738, 2.295, 60, $dyk$1836$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sainte-chapelle$dyk$, $dyk$Sainte-Chapelle$dyk$, $dyk$A jewel box of medieval glass$dyk$, $dyk$Hidden inside the Palais de Justice complex stands what many consider the most beautiful room in Europe. King Louis IX – the future Saint Louis – built Sainte-Chapelle in just seven years, consecrating it in 1248, for a very specific purpose: to house the Crown of Thorns, which he had purchased from the Byzantine emperor for a sum greater than the cost of the chapel itself. The building was conceived as a giant reliquary, a jewel box turned inside out. Step into the upper chapel and you'll understand: fifteen windows, each fifteen metres tall, dissolve the walls into 1,113 scenes of biblical stained glass, two-thirds of it still original from the 13th century. On a sunny day the entire room glows blue, red and gold, and the slender stone piers seem barely there at all – a gravity-defying feat of Gothic engineering. The chapel survived the French Revolution, when it was used to store archives, and the flooding of light was what saved it: the shelves blocked the lower windows, protecting the precious glass. The Crown of Thorns itself was moved to Notre-Dame, and survived the 2019 fire.$dyk$, 48.85541, 2.34503, 50, $dyk$1248$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$napoleons-tomb-invalides$dyk$, $dyk$Napoleon's Tomb, Les Invalides$dyk$, $dyk$The emperor's final resting place$dyk$, $dyk$Beneath the gleaming golden dome ahead lies the most controversial Frenchman who ever lived. Louis XIV built Les Invalides in the 1670s as a home for wounded war veterans – a role part of the complex still serves – but since 1861 its church has been dominated by one man: Napoleon Bonaparte. The emperor died in British exile on the remote island of Saint Helena in 1821, and for nineteen years he lay buried there under a nameless stone. In 1840 the French king ordered his remains brought home, an event known as the 'retour des cendres'. A million people lined the streets of Paris in freezing December weather as the funeral carriage passed. Napoleon now rests in a massive sarcophagus of red quartzite, placed in an open circular crypt so that – as visitors like to point out – you must bow your head to look down at him, paying respect whether you intend to or not. He lies inside six nested coffins, like a Russian doll: tin, mahogany, two of lead, ebony and oak. Around him rest his brothers, his son, and great marshals of France, including Foch of First World War fame.$dyk$, 48.85495, 2.31253, 70, $dyk$1861$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$jardin-du-luxembourg$dyk$, $dyk$Jardin du Luxembourg$dyk$, $dyk$The Parisians' favourite garden$dyk$, $dyk$Welcome to the garden Parisians love most. Marie de' Medici, the Italian-born widow of King Henri IV, created this park from 1612 around her new palace, built to remind her of the Pitti Palace of her native Florence. Today the palace houses the French Senate, and the 23 hectares around it belong, in every practical sense, to the people of Paris. Grab one of the famous green metal chairs – moveable, so you can chase the sun – and watch the city at leisure: students from the nearby Sorbonne reading on the terraces, old men playing chess under the chestnut trees, children pushing century-old wooden sailboats across the octagonal pond with sticks, just as their great-grandparents did. The garden contains over a hundred statues, an apple orchard with heritage varieties, beehives that have produced honey since 1856, and the Medici Fountain, a romantically shaded grotto that is one of the city's favourite kissing spots. Ernest Hemingway claimed that as a poor young writer he would wander here to catch pigeons for his dinner – strictly when the park police weren't looking.$dyk$, 48.84662, 2.33718, 80, $dyk$1612$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$musee-dorsay$dyk$, $dyk$Musée d'Orsay$dyk$, $dyk$Impressionism inside a railway station$dyk$, $dyk$The building before you is a giant railway station with no trains – and the finest collection of Impressionist art on the planet. The Gare d'Orsay was built for the 1900 World's Fair, an iron cathedral dressed in stone, so elegant that the painter Édouard Detaille joked it looked more like a palace of fine arts than a station. He turned out to be a prophet. By 1939 its platforms were too short for modern trains, and the station sank into a twilight existence as a mailing centre, a film set and a theatre. Demolition loomed in the 1970s, but France had just bulldozed the old Les Halles market and regretted it bitterly – so the station was saved and, in 1986, reborn as a museum bridging the gap between the Louvre and modern art. Under its magnificent glass vault, and behind the two giant station clocks, hang Monet's cathedrals, Renoir's dancing crowds, Van Gogh's swirling self-portraits, Degas's ballerinas and Cézanne's apples. Don't miss the view through the transparent clock face on the upper floor – Montmartre and Sacré-Cœur framed by the hands of time.$dyk$, 48.8599, 2.32657, 70, $dyk$1986$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$place-du-tertre$dyk$, $dyk$Place du Tertre, Montmartre$dyk$, $dyk$The painters' square on the hill$dyk$, $dyk$A few steps from the white domes of Sacré-Cœur lies a small village square that became the beating heart of modern art. Montmartre was an independent village of windmills and vineyards until Paris swallowed it in 1860, and its cheap rents soon drew penniless painters up the hill. Around this square and its surrounding streets lived and worked, at various times, Picasso, Van Gogh, Renoir, Toulouse-Lautrec, Modigliani and Utrillo. At the ramshackle Bateau-Lavoir studios nearby, Picasso painted Les Demoiselles d'Avignon in 1907 and ignited Cubism – art history's Big Bang, sparked a two-minute walk from where you stand. Today the square is filled with easel-toting artists who paint portraits and caricatures of visitors; the spots are so coveted that the city allocates numbered licences, and around 140 artists share the pitches, often two per space on alternating days. Yes, it's touristy – but sit down at a café terrace, order a coffee, and squint: between the easels and the plane trees, the village that changed painting forever is still there. A small vineyard, Clos Montmartre, still produces wine a few streets away.$dyk$, 48.88656, 2.3409, 50, $dyk$18th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$point-zero$dyk$, $dyk$Point Zéro$dyk$, $dyk$Where all roads in France begin$dyk$, $dyk$Look down. Somewhere near your feet, set into the cobblestones of the square in front of Notre-Dame, is a modest bronze-and-stone compass rose marked 'Point zéro des routes de France' – the official centre of the country. Since 1924 this small marker has been the point from which all road distances in France are measured. When a sign on a motorway says 'Paris 250 km', it means 250 kilometres to this exact spot. The location is no accident: the square in front of the cathedral has been the symbolic heart of Paris since the Middle Ages, and the Île de la Cité is where the city itself began as a settlement of the Parisii tribe over two thousand years ago. The marker has become wrapped in its own rituals. Tourists stand on it and spin on their heel, make wishes, kiss, or drop coins – depending on which superstition they've heard, doing so guarantees either good luck or a certain return to Paris one day. None of these traditions is official, all of them are cheerfully practised. It may be the most stepped-on monument in France – and most people walk over it without ever noticing.$dyk$, 48.85336, 2.34882, 40, $dyk$1924$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$nicolas-flamel-house$dyk$, $dyk$Nicolas Flamel's House$dyk$, $dyk$The alchemist's house from 1407$dyk$, $dyk$The stone house at 51 rue de Montmorency is the oldest in Paris – built in 1407 by a man Harry Potter fans will recognise instantly: Nicolas Flamel. In the books, Flamel is the alchemist who created the Philosopher's Stone and lived for over six centuries. The real Flamel was a successful Parisian scribe, manuscript dealer and property owner who died, rather conventionally, in 1418. So where did the legend come from? Flamel was unusually wealthy and unusually generous – he funded churches, chapels and housing for the poor, including this very house, where labourers could stay free provided they said prayers for the dead. Two centuries after his death, alchemical texts began to appear under his name, claiming his fortune had come from transmuting lead into gold. The story grew that Flamel and his wife Perenelle had faked their own deaths and were wandering the world, immortal. Treasure hunters repeatedly ransacked his houses and even his tombstone looking for hidden gold. Today the ground floor houses a restaurant – appropriately named Auberge Nicolas Flamel – where you can dine inside a genuine medieval legend.$dyk$, 48.8639, 2.3525, 40, $dyk$1407$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pont-des-arts$dyk$, $dyk$Pont des Arts$dyk$, $dyk$The bridge that sagged under love$dyk$, $dyk$This elegant pedestrian bridge linking the Louvre to the Institut de France was Paris's first iron bridge, opened in 1804 under Napoleon. But its worldwide fame came two centuries later – through padlocks. Around 2008, couples began attaching 'love locks' to the bridge's railings: write your names on a padlock, click it shut, and throw the key into the Seine to seal your love forever. The custom exploded. Within a few years an estimated 700,000 locks weighed down the railings – around 45 tonnes of metal, roughly the weight of ten elephants. In 2014, a section of railing actually collapsed under the load, and the city faced an unusual dilemma: how do you ban love? In 2015 the padlock-covered grilles were removed for good and replaced with glass panels, ending the tradition here – though hopeful romantics still try their luck on other bridges. The bridge has a gentler artistic legacy too: it has long served as an open-air studio for painters and photographers, and its benches offer one of the loveliest sunset views in Paris, straight down the Seine towards the Eiffel Tower.$dyk$, 48.85834, 2.3375, 50, $dyk$1804$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$palais-garnier-phantom$dyk$, $dyk$Palais Garnier$dyk$, $dyk$Home of the Phantom of the Opera$dyk$, $dyk$The opulent opera house before you – all gold, marble and sculpted nymphs – hides a darker legend beneath its stage: this is the home of the Phantom of the Opera. When Charles Garnier built his masterpiece, completed in 1875, engineers battled groundwater that kept flooding the foundations. Their solution was to create a huge water-filled concrete cistern beneath the building – the real 'underground lake' that novelist Gaston Leroux transformed into the Phantom's lair in his 1910 novel. Leroux wove real events into his tale. In 1896, one of the counterweights of the auditorium's grand chandelier actually fell during a performance, killing a concierge in seat 13 – in the novel, the Phantom sends the whole chandelier crashing down. Leroux insisted to his death that the Phantom had truly existed, and the opera keeps the myth alive: Box Five, the Phantom's box in the story, still bears a plaque reserving it for him. The underground water tank, meanwhile, is genuinely used by Paris firefighters for diving training, and a famous resident catfish is said to swim in its dark waters, fed by opera staff.$dyk$, 48.87187, 2.33167, 60, $dyk$1875$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$catacombs-of-paris$dyk$, $dyk$The Catacombs of Paris$dyk$, $dyk$The empire of the dead below$dyk$, $dyk$Twenty metres beneath your feet lies the largest grave in the world. The bones of an estimated six million Parisians – three times the city's living population today's central districts hold – are stacked in the tunnels below, arranged in walls of femurs and skulls. The story begins with a health catastrophe: by the 1780s the city's overflowing cemeteries were poisoning entire neighbourhoods, and in 1780 a cellar wall near the Innocents cemetery collapsed under the pressure of the dead. The solution was macabre and practical: from 1786, night-time processions of black-veiled carts transferred centuries of skeletons into the abandoned limestone quarries beneath the city – the very quarries whose stone had built Paris above. At the entrance to the ossuary a carved warning still greets visitors: 'Arrête! C'est ici l'empire de la mort' – Stop! This is the empire of death. Only a small section is open to the public; the full quarry network runs for nearly 300 kilometres, and a subculture of urban explorers called cataphiles roams it illegally. Police once discovered a fully equipped secret cinema, complete with bar, hidden in the tunnels.$dyk$, 48.83385, 2.33236, 50, $dyk$1786$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Paris$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pere-lachaise-jim-morrison$dyk$, $dyk$Père Lachaise Cemetery$dyk$, $dyk$The world's most visited cemetery$dyk$, $dyk$You are entering the most visited cemetery on Earth – a leafy city of the dead where over three million people come each year to wander among tombs of the famous. When Père Lachaise opened in 1804 it was considered too far from town, and Parisians snubbed it. The authorities staged a brilliant marketing coup: they transferred the remains of the beloved playwright Molière and the legendary medieval lovers Héloïse and Abélard here. Suddenly everyone wanted to be buried alongside celebrities, and the hillside filled with elaborate monuments. Today's residents include Oscar Wilde, whose art deco tomb was so covered in lipstick kisses that a glass barrier had to be installed; Frédéric Chopin, whose heart, per his own wish, was smuggled to Warsaw while his body stayed here; and Édith Piaf. The most pilgrimage-worn grave belongs to Jim Morrison of The Doors, who died in a Paris bathtub in 1971 aged 27. His grave has been guarded, fenced and repeatedly vandalised; a bust of him was stolen in 1988. Legends swirl that his death was faked – the cemetery's most persistent ghost story, kept alive by fans who leave whiskey, poems and cigarettes.$dyk$, 48.8614, 2.39325, 80, $dyk$1804$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$colosseum$dyk$, $dyk$The Colosseum$dyk$, $dyk$The arena of gladiators$dyk$, $dyk$The colossal ruin before you is the largest amphitheatre ever built – and nearly two thousand years after its opening games in the year 80, it remains the symbol of Rome itself. Emperor Vespasian began it on the drained lake of Nero's private palace, a pointed political gesture: land the tyrant had stolen for himself was returned to the people as entertainment. And what entertainment. Up to 50,000 spectators, seated strictly by social rank, watched gladiators fight, exotic animals hunted – and, in the early years, the arena flooded for staged naval battles. The opening festivities alone lasted a hundred days. Beneath the wooden arena floor lay the hypogeum, a two-storey underworld of tunnels, cages and 28 lift systems that could make wild beasts appear as if by magic. The building was an engineering marvel: 80 numbered entrances could fill or empty the entire arena in minutes – the same principle modern stadiums use today. Gladiators, by the way, rarely fought to the death; they were expensive investments, more sports stars than doomed slaves. Many had fans, sponsors and, if successful, eventual freedom.$dyk$, 41.89021, 12.49223, 80, $dyk$80$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pantheon$dyk$, $dyk$The Pantheon$dyk$, $dyk$The temple of all the gods$dyk$, $dyk$Step inside the best-preserved building of ancient Rome – and prepare for one of architecture's great gasps. The Pantheon, completed under Emperor Hadrian around the year 126, is crowned by a concrete dome that remained the largest in the world for over 1,300 years, and is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome ever built. Its secret lies in Roman engineering genius: the concrete gets progressively lighter towards the top, mixed with pumice, and the walls hide relieving arches that channel the colossal weight downwards. At the dome's centre is the oculus, a nine-metre-wide open eye to the sky – the building's only light source. Yes, rain falls through it; the sloping marble floor beneath has 22 discreet drainage holes, still working after nineteen centuries. The building survived because it never stopped being used: in 609 it was converted into a church, sparing it the fate of Rome's other temples, which were quarried for their stone. The Renaissance master Raphael chose to be buried here, and Michelangelo, studying the dome, declared it looked 'more like the work of angels than of men'.$dyk$, 41.89864, 12.47691, 60, $dyk$126$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$roman-forum$dyk$, $dyk$The Roman Forum$dyk$, $dyk$Downtown of the ancient world$dyk$, $dyk$The field of broken columns and triumphal arches below you was once the centre of the known world. For over a thousand years, the Roman Forum was the beating heart of the Republic and Empire – simultaneously parliament, supreme court, cathedral, marketplace and gossip exchange. Along the Via Sacra, the sacred road running through it, victorious generals paraded their triumphs, trailing chained kings and wagons of loot before roaring crowds. Here Cicero delivered the speeches that made rhetoric an art form; here Mark Antony gave Caesar's funeral oration as the crowd cremated the murdered dictator on an improvised pyre – the Temple of Caesar was built on the spot, and visitors still leave flowers on its altar. In the House of the Vestal Virgins lived the six priestesses who guarded Rome's eternal flame; their privileges were extraordinary, but letting the flame die meant flogging, and breaking their vow of chastity meant being buried alive. After Rome's fall, the Forum sank under twelve metres of silt and rubble, becoming a cow pasture known as Campo Vaccino. Systematic excavation only began in the 19th century – and continues today.$dyk$, 41.89252, 12.4852, 80, $dyk$7th century BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$st-peters-basilica$dyk$, $dyk$St Peter's Basilica$dyk$, $dyk$The largest church in the world$dyk$, $dyk$You are standing before the largest church in Christendom, built above what tradition holds is the grave of the apostle Peter, crucified in Nero's circus nearby around the year 64. The first basilica here was raised by Emperor Constantine in the fourth century; by the 1500s it was crumbling, and the popes launched the most ambitious building project of the Renaissance. It took 120 years, emptied the papal treasury – the sale of indulgences to fund it helped trigger Luther's Reformation – and consumed the genius of Bramante, Raphael, Michelangelo and Bernini. Michelangelo, in his seventies, designed the great dome, grumbling that he could make it 'bigger, but not more beautiful' than the Pantheon's. Inside, the scale plays tricks: the basilica is so vast, 187 metres long, that markers in the floor show where the world's other great cathedrals would end if placed inside it. Just past the entrance stands Michelangelo's Pietà, carved when he was 24 – the only work he ever signed, allegedly after overhearing visitors credit it to another sculptor. Excavations in the 1940s did indeed find a first-century grave directly beneath the high altar.$dyk$, 41.90216, 12.45391, 80, $dyk$1626$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sistine-chapel$dyk$, $dyk$The Sistine Chapel$dyk$, $dyk$Michelangelo's painted heaven$dyk$, $dyk$Inside the Vatican Museums awaits the most famous ceiling on Earth. When Pope Julius II ordered Michelangelo to fresco the Sistine Chapel's vault in 1508, the artist protested that he was a sculptor, not a painter – and suspected jealous rivals had arranged the commission hoping he would fail. Instead, over four agonising years, working on scaffolding of his own design with paint dripping into his eyes, he created a 500-square-metre universe: nine scenes from Genesis framed by prophets, sibyls and heroic nudes, over 300 figures in all. At its centre, God reaches out to Adam in the most reproduced gesture in art history – their fingers, famously, not quite touching. Twenty-two years later Michelangelo returned to paint The Last Judgment on the altar wall, scandalising the papal court with its swirling mass of naked bodies; after his death, a pupil was hired to paint draperies over the offending parts, earning the nickname 'Il Braghettone' – the breeches-maker. The chapel is also where cardinals lock themselves in to elect each new pope, the world watching for white smoke from its chimney.$dyk$, 41.90291, 12.45439, 50, $dyk$1512$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$caesar-assassination-site$dyk$, $dyk$Largo di Torre Argentina$dyk$, $dyk$Where Julius Caesar was murdered$dyk$, $dyk$The sunken square of ruins below street level is one of history's great crime scenes. On the Ides of March – the 15th – in 44 BC, Julius Caesar walked into the Curia of Pompey, a meeting hall whose remains stand at the edge of this excavation, for a session of the Senate. Sixty conspirators, led by Brutus and Cassius, were waiting with daggers hidden in their togas. They feared Caesar, recently declared dictator for life, was about to make himself king and end the Republic forever. Caesar was stabbed 23 times and fell, with bitter irony, at the foot of a statue of Pompey, his great defeated rival. Whether he really gasped 'Et tu, Brute?' is Shakespeare's invention; ancient sources suggest he said nothing, or perhaps a Greek phrase – 'You too, my child?' The assassination backfired spectacularly: instead of saving the Republic, it triggered civil wars that ended with Caesar's heir Augustus as Rome's first emperor. The ruins were only uncovered in the 1920s during construction work, and the exact spot has been identified where, according to sources, the dictator fell.$dyk$, 41.89555, 12.47663, 60, $dyk$44 BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$villa-borghese$dyk$, $dyk$Villa Borghese Gardens$dyk$, $dyk$Rome's green heart$dyk$, $dyk$Above the Spanish Steps spreads the park Romans call their green heart – 80 hectares of umbrella pines, fountains, temples and gardens where the city exhales. It began as the pleasure grounds of Cardinal Scipione Borghese, the art-obsessed nephew of a pope, who from 1606 transformed vineyards into the most lavish private park Rome had seen, completed with its villa by 1633. The cardinal used his palace here to house his staggering art collection – and his methods for acquiring it were themselves staggering: artists who refused to sell could find themselves imprisoned on invented charges. The city bought the whole estate in 1901, and Romans have treated it as their own living room ever since. Rent a rowing boat on the little lake with its picture-perfect Temple of Asclepius, hire a four-person pedal carriage, visit the tiny cinema showing films in their original language, or find the water clock of 1867 on the Pincio terrace. That terrace serves the classic Roman sunset: the domes of the city turning gold below you, with St Peter's silhouetted on the horizon. It is, by broad agreement, the most romantic view in Rome.$dyk$, 41.9123, 12.4853, 80, $dyk$1633$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$piazza-navona$dyk$, $dyk$Piazza Navona$dyk$, $dyk$Baroque theatre under the open sky$dyk$, $dyk$Notice the shape of this square: a long oval, unlike any other piazza in Rome. That's because you are standing on the racetrack of Emperor Domitian's stadium from the year 86 – the surrounding buildings rest on its ancient grandstands, and 'Navona' likely derives from the Greek word 'agones', meaning games. Today it is Rome's open-air baroque theatre, and its centrepiece is Bernini's Fountain of the Four Rivers from 1651: four muscular giants personifying the Nile, Danube, Ganges and Río de la Plata, crowned by an ancient Egyptian obelisk. Tour guides love telling you that the Plata figure raises his hand in horror at the church of Sant'Agnese, designed by Bernini's bitter rival Borromini – a delicious story, though sadly impossible, as the fountain was finished before the church façade was begun. The rivalry between the two geniuses, however, was entirely real. For centuries the square hosted markets, jousts and spectacles; in summer, the fountain outlets were once plugged to flood the piazza so the nobility could splash through in carriages. Grab a seat, order a (pricey) coffee, and watch the street artists work.$dyk$, 41.89902, 12.47307, 70, $dyk$1651$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$galleria-borghese$dyk$, $dyk$Galleria Borghese$dyk$, $dyk$Bernini's marble miracles$dyk$, $dyk$Inside this frescoed villa waits what many call the most beautiful small museum in the world. Cardinal Scipione Borghese built it in the early 1600s not as a home but as a showcase – a party palace for his art collection – and the collection remains substantially as he arranged it four centuries ago, opened to the public as a state museum in 1903. Its glory is Gian Lorenzo Bernini, the sculptor the cardinal discovered as a young prodigy. Here stands Apollo and Daphne, capturing the split second the fleeing nymph transforms into a laurel tree, her fingers sprouting marble leaves so thin they ring when struck; The Rape of Proserpina, where Pluto's fingers press into the girl's thigh as if the stone were flesh; and David, biting his lip in concentration – a self-portrait of Bernini, who had a cardinal hold a mirror for him while he carved. Alongside them hang six Caravaggios, including the painter's eerie David with the Head of Goliath, where Goliath's severed head is Caravaggio's own face. Visits are strictly limited to two-hour time slots, so book well ahead – the marble is worth the planning.$dyk$, 41.91424, 12.49218, 60, $dyk$1903$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$trevi-fountain$dyk$, $dyk$Trevi Fountain$dyk$, $dyk$Toss a coin, return to Rome$dyk$, $dyk$Squeezed into a tiny square, the Trevi Fountain is deliberately overwhelming: a whole palace wall transformed into a marble sea, where Oceanus rides a shell chariot drawn by winged horses through crashing artificial cliffs. Completed in 1762 after thirty years of work, it marks the end point of the Aqua Virgo, an aqueduct built in 19 BC that still feeds Rome's fountains after two thousand years. The name Trevi comes from 'tre vie' – three roads – which met here. The famous ritual: stand with your back to the fountain and toss a coin with your right hand over your left shoulder. One coin guarantees your return to Rome; two, says the extended version, bring new romance; three, a wedding. Tourists throw in roughly 1.5 million euros a year, all of it collected regularly and donated to the Catholic charity Caritas to help Rome's poor – and yes, fishing coins out yourself is illegal. The fountain owes much of its fame to cinema: Anita Ekberg wading through it in a black dress in Fellini's La Dolce Vita created one of film history's most iconic scenes. Don't copy her – wading carries a hefty fine.$dyk$, 41.9009, 12.48326, 50, $dyk$1762$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$aventine-keyhole$dyk$, $dyk$The Aventine Keyhole$dyk$, $dyk$The most famous keyhole in the world$dyk$, $dyk$On the quiet Aventine Hill, in a peaceful square designed by the artist Piranesi in 1765, stands a heavy green door with an unremarkable brass keyhole. Bend down, press your eye to it – and gasp. Through the keyhole, a tunnel of perfectly trimmed garden hedges frames the dome of St Peter's Basilica, floating in the distance like a vision. It is perhaps the world's only keyhole with a guaranteed masterpiece view, and on busy days a patient queue forms at the door for a few seconds of peeping each. The door belongs to the Priory of the Knights of Malta, one of the world's oldest chivalric orders, founded during the Crusades around 1048. The Order is a genuine curiosity of international law: it has no country, yet issues its own passports, stamps and licence plates, and holds diplomatic relations with over a hundred states – making it arguably the smallest sovereign entity on Earth. Whether the keyhole view was designed intentionally remains debated, which only improves the mystery. The trick, locals say, is to visit at dawn, when the light is soft and the queue hasn't formed.$dyk$, 41.88243, 12.47672, 40, $dyk$1765$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$torre-argentina-cat-sanctuary$dyk$, $dyk$Torre Argentina Cat Sanctuary$dyk$, $dyk$Cats living in Caesar's ruins$dyk$, $dyk$Look down into the sunken ruins where Julius Caesar was assassinated and you'll spot the site's current rulers: cats. Dozens of them drape themselves over 2,000-year-old column stumps, sunbathe on sacred temple steps and patrol the excavation with complete indifference to its historical gravity. Rome has always had street cats – they're protected by law as part of the city's 'bio-cultural heritage', and a 1991 regulation grants them the right to live undisturbed wherever they were born. When the Largo di Torre Argentina was excavated in the 1920s, cats moved in immediately, and volunteer 'gattare' – cat ladies – have fed them ever since. The most famous gattara of an earlier era was the opera diva Anna Magnani, who fed strays between takes at the nearby theatre. Since 1993 an official sanctuary has operated in a corner of the ruins, sheltering, sterilising and rehoming hundreds of cats yearly, funded entirely by donations and run by volunteers. Visitors are welcome. There is something perfectly Roman about it: the spot where an empire's fate turned now belongs to sleeping cats.$dyk$, 41.8954, 12.4769, 40, $dyk$1993$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$mouth-of-truth$dyk$, $dyk$The Mouth of Truth$dyk$, $dyk$The lie detector of ancient Rome$dyk$, $dyk$In the portico of the church of Santa Maria in Cosmedin hangs a huge, weathered marble disc carved with the bearded face of a river god – mouth open, waiting. This is the Bocca della Verità, the Mouth of Truth, and the legend is simple and chilling: put your hand in the mouth and tell a lie, and the mouth bites your hand off. The stone itself is ancient, likely a first-century drain cover or fountain decoration, but its career as a lie detector began in the Middle Ages, when it was reportedly used in trials – suspects swore oaths with a hand in the mouth. The most delicious medieval tale tells of a noblewoman accused of adultery who arranged for her lover, disguised as a madman, to embrace her in front of the crowd just before her test. She could then truthfully swear that no man had ever held her except her husband and 'that poor lunatic' – and the mouth, outwitted, spared her hand. One version adds that priests with scorpions behind the disc handled less clever liars. The stone became a global celebrity in 1953, when Gregory Peck pranked Audrey Hepburn here in Roman Holiday – her shriek was unscripted.$dyk$, 41.8882, 12.48146, 40, $dyk$1st century$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$capitoline-wolf$dyk$, $dyk$The Capitoline Wolf$dyk$, $dyk$The she-wolf who nursed Rome$dyk$, $dyk$On the Capitoline Hill, in the museums designed by Michelangelo, stands the bronze she-wolf who is the mother of Rome itself. The legend: the god Mars fathered twin boys, Romulus and Remus, with a priestess. A usurping king, fearing their claim, ordered the babies drowned in the Tiber – but the basket washed ashore, where a she-wolf found the twins and suckled them in a cave called the Lupercal, at the foot of the Palatine Hill nearby. Raised by a shepherd, the brothers grew up to found a city on the spot where they were saved. Then came history's most fateful sibling quarrel: arguing over which hill to build on and who the gods favoured, Romulus killed Remus and named the city after himself. Rome's traditional birthday – 21 April 753 BC – is still celebrated every year. The statue itself carries a modern twist: long believed to be Etruscan work from around 500 BC, radiocarbon tests in 2007 suggested the wolf may actually be medieval, cast in the 13th century. The debate rages on. The suckling twins beneath her are Renaissance additions – probably by the workshop of Pollaiolo.$dyk$, 41.89306, 12.48278, 50, $dyk$c. 500 BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Rome$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pasquino$dyk$, $dyk$Pasquino, the Talking Statue$dyk$, $dyk$Rome's stone satirist since 1501$dyk$, $dyk$The battered, noseless torso on this corner doesn't look like much – but for five centuries, this has been the most dangerous statue in Rome. Pasquino is an ancient sculpture, probably showing Menelaus from the Trojan War, dug up and placed here in 1501. Soon after, anonymous Romans began pinning verses to him at night: poems mocking popes, cardinals and princes, written in sharp Roman dialect. The statue 'spoke' what no living person dared say aloud, and the practice became so famous it gave the world a word – 'pasquinade', a published lampoon. The authorities were not amused. Pope Adrian VI reportedly wanted Pasquino thrown into the Tiber, and one adviser talked him out of it with a warning: like a frog, the statue would only croak louder from the water. Instead, guards watched the statue at night – so Romans simply recruited other statues, creating a whole 'Congress of Witty Statues' that answered one another across the city. When Napoleon's troops looted Rome's art, Pasquino asked: 'It's true the French are all thieves?' Another statue replied: 'Not all – but a good part' ('Buonaparte'). Notes still appear on Pasquino today.$dyk$, 41.8979, 12.4718, 40, $dyk$1501$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sagrada-familia$dyk$, $dyk$Sagrada Família$dyk$, $dyk$Gaudí's unfinished masterpiece$dyk$, $dyk$The forest of towers rising above you is the most famous construction site on Earth. Work on the Sagrada Família began in 1882, and Antoni Gaudí took over the project a year later, aged just 31. He would devote the rest of his life to it – his final years spent living in the workshop, dressed so shabbily that when a tram struck him in 1926, passers-by mistook the great architect for a beggar and he received only basic care before dying. He knew he would never see it finished: 'My client', he shrugged, meaning God, 'is not in a hurry.' Gaudí designed the basilica as a Bible told in stone – the Nativity façade bubbles with life, sculpted plants and animals, while the later Passion façade is stripped and skeletal like grief itself. Inside, columns branch like trees into a canopy of geometry and coloured light; Gaudí derived the forms from nature, testing his structures with famous upside-down models of hanging chains. Construction, funded entirely by donations and entry tickets as Gaudí insisted, has now passed 140 years. The central Tower of Jesus will make it the tallest church in the world upon completion.$dyk$, 41.40363, 2.17436, 80, $dyk$1882$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$casa-batllo$dyk$, $dyk$Casa Batlló$dyk$, $dyk$The house of bones and dragons$dyk$, $dyk$Locals call it the House of Bones, and one look tells you why: the balconies stare like skulls, and the pillars of the lower floor curve like tibias. Casa Batlló is what happened when the textile magnate Josep Batlló gave Antoni Gaudí complete creative freedom in 1904 to remodel a dull apartment block on Barcelona's smartest avenue. Gaudí, at the peak of his powers, delivered a building with hardly a straight line in it. The scaly roof arches like the back of a sleeping dragon, and the turret with its cross is popularly read as the lance of Sant Jordi – Saint George, Catalonia's patron saint – plunged into the beast, with the skull-balconies as the dragon's victims. Gaudí never explained his symbolism, which keeps the theories delightfully alive. Inside, the obsession with detail continues: the light well is tiled in five shades of blue, darker at the top and paler below, so every floor receives the same quality of light; doors and windows ripple like water; even the door handles were sculpted to fit a grasping hand perfectly. The building sits on the so-called 'Block of Discord', where rival modernista architects built competing showpieces side by side.$dyk$, 41.39165, 2.16496, 50, $dyk$1906$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$barcelona-cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Barcelona Cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Gothic heart of the old city$dyk$, $dyk$The Gothic façade towering over you looks medieval – but it's a clever late arrival. The cathedral itself was built mainly between 1298 and 1448, yet for over four centuries it presented a plain, unfinished front to the city, because funds ran out. Only in the 1880s, when a wealthy industrialist offered to pay, was the intricate neo-Gothic façade added, following a design from 1408 – making the 'newest' part of the building its most photographed. The cathedral is dedicated to Santa Eulàlia, Barcelona's young co-patron saint, martyred by the Romans around the year 303 at the age of thirteen, and her alabaster tomb rests in the crypt beneath the high altar. The building's most charming corner is its cloister: a leafy Gothic courtyard with palm trees, a mossy fountain – and thirteen white geese, one for each year of Eulàlia's short life. Geese have been kept here for centuries; besides their symbolic duty, they historically doubled as an alarm system, honking furiously at intruders. During the fountain festival of Corpus Christi, an emptied eggshell is set dancing on the fountain's jet – the beloved tradition of 'l'ou com balla', the dancing egg.$dyk$, 41.38396, 2.17624, 60, $dyk$1448$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$santa-maria-del-mar$dyk$, $dyk$Santa Maria del Mar$dyk$, $dyk$The people's cathedral by the sea$dyk$, $dyk$This is the church Barcelona's people built for themselves. While bishops and kings raised the official cathedral across town, the merchants, sailors and dockworkers of the wealthy Ribera district wanted their own – and between 1329 and 1383 they created what many consider the finest Gothic church in Spain. The speed was extraordinary: just 54 years, which is why the building has a rare purity of style, untouched by changing fashions. The stone was hauled from the royal quarry on Montjuïc hill by the bastaixos, the port's stevedores, who carried massive blocks on their backs across the city as their contribution to the parish. They are immortalised in small reliefs on the main doors – working men given the place of honour usually reserved for saints. Inside, the church is a revelation: three soaring naves of bare stone, slender octagonal columns spaced wider than in any other Gothic church, and light everywhere. The bareness has a dark cause – anarchists torched the church in 1936 during the Civil War, and the fire burned for eleven days, consuming the baroque clutter of centuries. What survived is pure architecture. The novel 'Cathedral of the Sea' made the church's construction story a worldwide bestseller.$dyk$, 41.3838, 2.182, 60, $dyk$1383$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$picasso-frieze-architects-college$dyk$, $dyk$The Picasso Frieze$dyk$, $dyk$Picasso's giant public doodle$dyk$, $dyk$Look up at the modern building facing the cathedral square: the childlike figures dancing across its concrete frieze were drawn by Pablo Picasso. In the early 1960s, the Architects' College of Catalonia commissioned the world's most famous living artist – a son of Barcelona in spirit, having spent his formative youth here – to decorate its new headquarters. Picasso, then in his eighties and exiled in France, sent drawings depicting Catalan popular festivals: giants, children with palm branches, dancing figures. Since Picasso couldn't travel to fascist Spain, the Norwegian artist Carl Nesjar transferred the designs to the façade by sandblasting lines into the concrete. The result, unveiled in 1962, was Picasso's largest public artwork – and a quiet act of defiance. Picasso had vowed never to set foot in Spain while Franco ruled, and Franco's regime despised him; his Guernica, the century's great anti-fascist painting, was banned from the country. Yet here were his joyful Catalan children, dancing across a façade in the heart of Barcelona, facing down the Gothic quarter. Picasso never saw it in person – he died in 1973, two years before Franco, and never returned to Spain.$dyk$, 41.3847, 2.1751, 40, $dyk$1962$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$columbus-monument$dyk$, $dyk$The Columbus Monument$dyk$, $dyk$Pointing the wrong way to America$dyk$, $dyk$At the foot of La Rambla, a 60-metre column lifts Christopher Columbus into the Mediterranean sky, his arm thrust dramatically seaward. The monument rose in 1888 for Barcelona's Universal Exposition, celebrating the moment the city claims as its own: in 1493, returning from his first Atlantic voyage, Columbus was received here by the Catholic Monarchs, Ferdinand and Isabella, reportedly presenting them with treasures and native Taíno people from the Caribbean in the courtyard of the royal palace in the Gothic quarter. There's a popular puzzle about the statue: Columbus appears to point towards America – except he doesn't. His finger aims east-southeast, roughly towards the Mediterranean and, at best, Genoa in Italy, his probable birthplace – not west across the Atlantic. Guides offer competing explanations: he points to the sea in general, to the route home, or simply in the direction that looked best from La Rambla. Inside the iron column, a small lift installed in the 1920s carries visitors up to a cramped viewing gallery beneath Columbus's feet, with panoramic views over the port and the old city. In recent years the monument has become a focus of debate about the darker legacy of the man it celebrates.$dyk$, 41.37577, 2.17766, 50, $dyk$1888$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$park-guell$dyk$, $dyk$Park Güell$dyk$, $dyk$Gaudí's mosaic wonderland$dyk$, $dyk$The fairy-tale terraces above the city began as a real-estate flop. Count Eusebi Güell, Gaudí's great patron, dreamed of an exclusive English-style garden suburb – hence 'Park' in English – with sixty villas on this hillside. Gaudí designed the infrastructure between 1900 and 1914: gatehouses like gingerbread cottages, a grand stairway guarded by a mosaic salamander, a hall of 86 leaning columns, and the famous serpentine bench, possibly the longest in the world, coiling around the main terrace. Then reality intervened: the plots were remote, expensive and bound by strict building rules, and precisely two of the sixty were ever sold. One buyer was Gaudí himself, who lived in the show house – now his museum – for twenty years. The commercial failure became the city's gain when Barcelona bought the site as a public park in 1922. Look closely at the surfaces: the shattered-tile technique, trencadís, was partly recycling – Gaudí's workers collected broken tiles from factories, and legend says workmen smashed whole tiles to meet the master's demand for fragments. The bench's ergonomic curve was reportedly moulded from the imprint of a worker sitting in wet plaster.$dyk$, 41.41449, 2.15268, 80, $dyk$1914$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$la-boqueria$dyk$, $dyk$La Boqueria Market$dyk$, $dyk$Barcelona's temple of food$dyk$, $dyk$Step off La Rambla under the stained-glass sign and into a cathedral of food. La Boqueria is Barcelona's oldest and most famous market – traders have sold here since medieval times, when farmers and butchers set up tables by the old city gate to dodge town taxes. The present iron-and-glass structure took shape from 1840 onwards, built on the site of a demolished convent, and today some 200 stalls pack its halls. The front rows nearest La Rambla cater to tourists with technicolor fruit juices and cone-cups of jamón; push deeper and you find the working market – fishmongers arranging octopus like sculpture, butchers selling every conceivable part of the pig, mushroom specialists, egg stalls stacked with a dozen varieties. The market's soul is its bars: tiny counters like the famous El Quim and Pinotxo, where market workers, chefs and lucky visitors squeeze onto stools for some of the best simple cooking in Spain – baby squid with white beans, salt cod fritters, whatever came in that morning. Michelin-starred chefs shop here daily; spotting them arguing over produce is a local sport. Come before ten in the morning to see it at its authentic best.$dyk$, 41.38164, 2.17151, 60, $dyk$1840$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$barceloneta-beach$dyk$, $dyk$Barceloneta Beach$dyk$, $dyk$The city beach born of the Olympics$dyk$, $dyk$Here's a secret hiding in plain sight: Barcelona's beloved beaches are younger than many of the people lying on them. For most of the 20th century, this shoreline was an industrial mess of warehouses, rail tracks and fishing shacks – the city famously 'lived with its back to the sea'. Everything changed with the 1992 Olympic Games. In a burst of urban reinvention that city planners worldwide still study, Barcelona demolished the industrial barrier, imported sand, built promenades and opened four kilometres of new city beaches. Barceloneta, fronting the old fishermen's quarter of the same name, became the star. The neighbourhood behind it dates from the 1750s, built on a grid of narrow streets to rehouse families displaced when the citadel was built; it kept its salty, working-class soul, and its streets still smell of grilled fish at lunchtime. Today the beach is one of Europe's great urban playgrounds: swimmers and volleyball players, the shimmering Frank Gehry fish sculpture at one end, beach bars serving cold cava, and the W Hotel's sail silhouette closing the view. In summer, the crowd density rivals the metro at rush hour – locals prefer May, June and September.$dyk$, 41.37847, 2.1924, 80, $dyk$1992$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$miro-mosaic-la-rambla$dyk$, $dyk$The Miró Mosaic, La Rambla$dyk$, $dyk$A masterpiece underfoot$dyk$, $dyk$Thousands of people walk across a genuine Joan Miró every day – most without noticing. Halfway down La Rambla, at the Pla de l'Os, the pavement erupts into a circular mosaic of bold red, yellow, blue and white: a work laid in 1976 by Miró himself, one of the giants of 20th-century art and a proud son of Barcelona, born a few streets away in the Gothic quarter. Miró wanted art out of museums and into daily life, and gave the city three works to greet travellers: a ceramic mural at the airport for those arriving by plane, a sculpture near the port for those arriving by sea, and this pavement for those arriving on foot down La Rambla. He deliberately placed it to be stepped on – art as street, street as art. One tile bears his signature; finding it is a beloved local game. The spot itself is historic: Pla de l'Os was the site of one of the medieval city gates, where travellers first entered Barcelona. After decades of shoes, suitcase wheels and festival crowds, the mosaic was restored in the 2000s, and the city now maintains it as carefully as any canvas in a gallery.$dyk$, 41.3808, 2.1731, 40, $dyk$1976$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$font-de-canaletes$dyk$, $dyk$Font de Canaletes$dyk$, $dyk$Drink here and you'll return$dyk$, $dyk$The ornate cast-iron fountain at the top of La Rambla, crowned with four spouts and lamps, dispenses two things: ordinary drinking water and a guarantee. As the plaque at its base declares, whoever drinks from the Font de Canaletes will fall in love with Barcelona and always return. The legend likely began as clever marketing by hotel staff and guides in the early 20th century, but generations have cheerfully complied, and the queue of sippers is constant. The fountain has a second, louder life: it is the official celebration spot of FC Barcelona. The tradition began in the 1930s, when the offices of a sports newspaper stood nearby; fans gathered under its windows to hear football results chalked up on a board, and when Barça won, the party started at the fountain. The newspaper is long gone, but the ritual is unbreakable – every league title, cup and Champions League trophy brings tens of thousands of delirious fans converging on this exact spot, climbing the lampposts and singing until dawn. On quiet days it's just a pretty fountain with cold water. On winning nights, it's the loudest place in Catalonia.$dyk$, 41.38538, 2.1701, 40, $dyk$19th century$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$gaudi-lampposts-placa-reial$dyk$, $dyk$Gaudí's Lampposts, Plaça Reial$dyk$, $dyk$The master's first commission$dyk$, $dyk$Before the Sagrada Família, before Park Güell, before the world knew his name – Antoni Gaudí designed streetlights. In the palm-shaded elegance of Plaça Reial, just off La Rambla, stand two six-armed lampposts that were the very first public commission of the man who would become architecture's great visionary. Gaudí was in his mid-twenties, freshly graduated, when the city hired him in 1878; the lamps were installed the following year. Look closely and the future master is already visible: the arms curl with botanical grace, and the tops are crowned by the winged helmet and serpent staff of Mercury, Roman god of commerce – a nod to Barcelona's merchant soul. The city paid him, the story goes, less than he'd hoped, and Gaudí never took another commission from the municipality; whether from pride or coincidence, the world's most famous Catalan architect built everything else for private patrons and the church. When Gaudí graduated, the school's director famously remarked: 'We have given this academic title either to a fool or a genius. Time will show.' The square around the lamps, by the way, is one of Barcelona's liveliest – arcaded, palm-filled, and buzzing until very late.$dyk$, 41.3801, 2.1752, 50, $dyk$1879$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pont-del-bisbe$dyk$, $dyk$El Pont del Bisbe$dyk$, $dyk$The bridge with a skull secret$dyk$, $dyk$The lace-like stone bridge arching over Carrer del Bisbe is the most photographed corner of the Gothic Quarter – and a beautiful fake with a dark garnish. It looks medieval, but was built in 1928 by the architect Joan Rubió, a disciple of Gaudí, when the neighbourhood was being romantically 'gothicised' for the upcoming International Exposition. Rubió had actually proposed a far grander scheme to rebuild whole streets in neo-Gothic style; the city approved only this small bridge connecting the Government Palace to the president's residence. What gives the bridge its legend lies underneath: look up as you pass beneath, and you'll find a carved skull pierced by a dagger. Stories bloomed around it almost immediately. One says Rubió, bitter that his grand plans were rejected, placed the skull as a curse upon the city. Another claims that removing the dagger from the skull would make Barcelona crumble – or, in cheerier versions, that walking beneath while looking at the skull brings bad luck, unless you walk backwards, which undoes it. The prosaic truth – that memento mori skulls were common Gothic ornaments – has never stood a chance against the legends.$dyk$, 41.384, 2.1759, 40, $dyk$1928$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$santa-eulalia-descent$dyk$, $dyk$Baixada de Santa Eulàlia$dyk$, $dyk$The martyr of thirteen torments$dyk$, $dyk$This sloping lane in the Gothic Quarter carries the name of Barcelona's own child saint – and one of the city's darkest legends. Eulàlia was, tradition says, a thirteen-year-old Christian girl living under Roman rule around the year 303, when Emperor Diocletian's persecution of Christians reached Barcino. She confronted the Roman governor over the cruelty, and the enraged official subjected her to thirteen tortures – one for each year of her life. The most famous torment gives this street its name: Eulàlia was sealed inside a barrel studded with knives and rolled down this very slope, the 'baixada', or descent. She survived, the legend continues, torment after torment – flames turned away from her, and when she finally died on a cross, a white dove flew from her mouth and a sudden snowfall covered her body like a shroud. A small niche with her image watches over the lane today. Eulàlia became Barcelona's patron saint, her relics rest in the cathedral crypt, and the thirteen geese in the cathedral cloister honour her thirteen years. Every February the city celebrates her festival with giants, human towers and processions – a party thrown for a Roman-era teenager.$dyk$, 41.3831, 2.1752, 40, $dyk$4th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Barcelona$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$placa-sant-felip-neri$dyk$, $dyk$Plaça de Sant Felip Neri$dyk$, $dyk$The scarred square of quiet echoes$dyk$, $dyk$Hidden behind the cathedral lies the Gothic Quarter's most haunting corner: a small, shaded square with a single octagonal fountain, an old church – and walls pockmarked with scars. On 30 January 1938, at the height of the Civil War, a bomb dropped by Franco's allied Italian aircraft exploded here. Refugee children were sheltering in the church basement; 42 people died, most of them children, and moments later a second bomb struck as rescuers worked. The shrapnel wounds are still visible across the church façade, left deliberately unrepaired as a silent memorial. For decades a darker rumour circulated – that the marks came from executions by firing squad against the wall. That version is false, but it spread so widely that a plaque now explains the true, hardly less tragic history. The square has other stories layered into it: it was built over a medieval cemetery, and the buildings around it include guild houses moved here stone by stone when other streets were demolished. Antoni Gaudí was walking to this church, where he attended mass daily, when the tram struck him in 1926. Today a school occupies part of the square, and children's laughter at break time fills a space that lost so many – the city's gentlest act of remembrance.$dyk$, 41.3829, 2.1745, 40, $dyk$1938$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$brandenburg-gate$dyk$, $dyk$Brandenburg Gate$dyk$, $dyk$Germany's gate of history$dyk$, $dyk$No monument has watched more German history than the sandstone gate before you. Built in 1791 as a customs gate crowned with the Quadriga – a four-horse chariot driven by the goddess of victory – it was intended as a gate of peace. History had other plans. Napoleon marched through in 1806 and liked the Quadriga so much he shipped it to Paris as war booty; it returned eight years later after his defeat, and Berliners joked the goddess was the only Parisian ever to move to Berlin voluntarily. Prussian troops, Nazi torch parades, Soviet tanks – all passed this way. When the Wall went up in 1961, the gate stood stranded in the death strip between East and West, accessible to no one, and became the symbol of divided Europe. Here Ronald Reagan demanded in 1987: 'Mr Gorbachev, tear down this wall!' Two years later, the world watched jubilant crowds swarm over the Wall at this very spot. When the gate was restored afterwards, workers found the Quadriga riddled with wartime bullet holes. Today it is Germany's symbol of unity – and the place where the nation gathers to celebrate.$dyk$, 52.51627, 13.3777, 70, $dyk$1791$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$reichstag$dyk$, $dyk$The Reichstag$dyk$, $dyk$Parliament of fire and glass$dyk$, $dyk$Few buildings embody a nation's turbulence like the Reichstag. Opened in 1894 to house the German parliament, its facade bears the dedication 'Dem Deutschen Volke' – To the German People, added during the First World War. In February 1933, a fire gutted its plenary chamber; the Nazis blamed a communist plot, and within a day Hitler was granted emergency powers that dismantled German democracy. Historians still argue over who actually lit the fire. In 1945, the building became the trophy of the Battle of Berlin – the famous photograph of a Soviet soldier raising the red flag over its roof became the war's defining image, and Soviet soldiers' graffiti, scrawled in Cyrillic on the walls, is preserved inside behind glass. After decades of Cold War slumber beside the Wall, the Reichstag was reborn for the reunified nation. Before renovation, the artists Christo and Jeanne-Claude wrapped the entire building in silver fabric in 1995 – five million people came to see it. The British architect Norman Foster then crowned it with a glass dome where citizens literally walk above the heads of their politicians: transparency turned into architecture. Visits are free but must be booked ahead.$dyk$, 52.51863, 13.3762, 80, $dyk$1894$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$checkpoint-charlie$dyk$, $dyk$Checkpoint Charlie$dyk$, $dyk$The Cold War's front door$dyk$, $dyk$The little white guardhouse in the middle of Friedrichstrasse – a replica of the original – marks the most famous border crossing of the Cold War. Checkpoint Charlie, named simply for the letter C in the NATO alphabet after checkpoints Alpha and Bravo, was the crossing reserved for Allied personnel, diplomats and foreigners passing between West and East Berlin from 1961. In October of that year, a dispute over an American diplomat's theatre visit escalated until American and Soviet tanks rolled up and faced each other here, barrels loaded, thirty metres apart – sixteen hours in which a wrong move could have started a third world war, until both sides quietly backed away. The checkpoint became the stage for desperate escapes: East Germans hidden in modified cars, false documents, dashes through the barriers. It was also where 18-year-old Peter Fechter, shot during an escape attempt in 1962, bled to death at the foot of the Wall while the world's press photographed helplessly – a death that came to symbolise the Wall's brutality. The famous sign – 'You are leaving the American sector' – remains one of history's most photographed warnings.$dyk$, 52.50754, 13.39039, 50, $dyk$1961$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$east-side-gallery$dyk$, $dyk$East Side Gallery$dyk$, $dyk$The Wall turned into art$dyk$, $dyk$The 1.3 kilometres of concrete along the Spree before you form the longest surviving stretch of the Berlin Wall – and the longest open-air gallery in the world. In the euphoric spring of 1990, months after the Wall fell, 118 artists from 21 countries were invited to paint the eastern side – a side that had been unreachable, and unpaintable, for 28 years while the Wall stood; approaching it from the East meant risking being shot. The artists turned the instrument of division into a canvas of freedom. The most famous image needs no introduction: Dmitri Vrubel's 'Fraternal Kiss', showing Soviet leader Brezhnev and East Germany's Honecker locked in a mouth-to-mouth embrace – based on a real photograph from 1979, when such socialist fraternal kisses were official protocol. Nearby, Birgit Kinder's Trabant car bursts through the concrete with the licence plate 'NOV 9-89', the night the Wall opened. Weather, exhaust and souvenir hunters chipping keepsakes have forced repeated restorations, with many artists returning to repaint their own works. The gallery is now a protected memorial – ironic immortality for a structure its builders called an 'anti-fascist protection rampart' and its victims simply called the Wall.$dyk$, 52.50505, 13.4397, 80, $dyk$1990$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$holocaust-memorial$dyk$, $dyk$Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe$dyk$, $dyk$A field of silence in the city's heart$dyk$, $dyk$A city block from the Brandenburg Gate, 2,711 grey concrete blocks ripple across an entire field – Germany's national memorial to the six million Jews murdered in the Holocaust, opened in 2005 after seventeen years of debate. The American architect Peter Eisenman designed the stelae in varying heights on undulating ground, and deliberately refused to explain what they mean. The number 2,711 has no symbolism, he insisted; there is no assigned meaning at all. The experience is the meaning: walk into the grid and the blocks rise around you, the city's sounds fade, the ground tilts unpredictably, and a strange uneasy disorientation sets in – order and madness at once. Some visitors see a graveyard, others a wheat field or a frozen sea; Eisenman accepted all readings. Beneath the field, an information centre documents the victims with names and family histories, including a room that reads aloud names and biographies of the murdered – reading all known names would take more than six years. The memorial's location is itself pointed: this was the death strip of the Wall, and the bunker where Goebbels' ministry operated lay nearby. Germany placed the memory of its greatest crime in the most prominent spot it had.$dyk$, 52.51392, 13.37866, 80, $dyk$2005$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$frederick-the-great-statue$dyk$, $dyk$Frederick the Great Statue$dyk$, $dyk$Old Fritz on his high horse$dyk$, $dyk$Riding high above Unter den Linden is 'Old Fritz' – Frederick the Great, the Prussian king who made Berlin a European capital. The equestrian bronze, unveiled in 1851 after decades of planning, shows the king in his everyday uniform and famous tricorne hat, while around the massive plinth swirl life-sized figures of his generals, ministers and thinkers – with the horsemen given the corners and, as wits noted, the philosophers placed under the horse's tail. Frederick ruled from 1740 to 1786 and was a walking contradiction: a French-speaking flute virtuoso and philosopher who corresponded with Voltaire, wrote essays against Machiavelli, and also waged wars that made Prussia a great power. He introduced religious tolerance, abolished torture, and campaigned so successfully for the potato that grateful Germans still lay potatoes on his grave in Potsdam today. The statue itself has a Cold War tale: the communist East German government, uncomfortable with Prussian militarism, removed the monument in 1950 and exiled it to Potsdam. But by 1980, seeking historical legitimacy, the regime rehabilitated the old king and returned him to his avenue – proof that in Berlin, even statues have complicated biographies.$dyk$, 52.51706, 13.39385, 40, $dyk$1851$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tiergarten$dyk$, $dyk$The Tiergarten$dyk$, $dyk$Berlin's great green lung$dyk$, $dyk$The vast park spreading around you is older than most nations: first fenced as a royal hunting ground in 1527 – Tiergarten means 'animal garden' – it was opened to the public in the 18th century and landscaped into 210 hectares of winding paths, meadows and hidden gardens, larger than London's Hyde Park. Its history mirrors the city's. In the Nazi years, the grand avenue through it was widened for planned imperial parades. Then came 1945: in the desperate winters after the war, freezing Berliners chopped down virtually every tree in the park for firewood and turned the lawns into potato patches. Photographs from 1946 show a moonscape of stumps stretching to the horizon. The park you see is thus a deliberate act of rebirth – replanted from 1949 onwards, with trees donated by cities across West Germany. Today the Tiergarten is Berlin's democratic backyard: joggers, picnicking families, sunbathers (in places, traditionally nude – this is Berlin), beer gardens like the lakeside Café am Neuen See, and the golden Victory Column at its heart, where Berliners gather for festivals and raves alike. Statesmen from the nearby government quarter take their confidential walks here too.$dyk$, 52.5145, 13.3501, 80, $dyk$1527$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$museum-island$dyk$, $dyk$Museum Island$dyk$, $dyk$Five museums, one island$dyk$, $dyk$In the middle of the Spree river lies an island devoted entirely to art and archaeology – five monumental museums built between 1830 and 1930, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Prussian kings conceived it as a 'sanctuary of art and science', beginning with the Altes Museum, whose rotunda was modelled on the Pantheon in Rome. The island's superstar is Egyptian: the 3,400-year-old bust of Queen Nefertiti in the Neues Museum, discovered in 1912 and considered one of the most beautiful portraits ever made – her painted gaze so lifelike that the excavator wrote in his diary, 'Suddenly we had the most alive Egyptian artwork in our hands. You cannot describe it. You must see it.' Egypt has requested her return ever since. The Pergamon Museum houses entire ancient structures – the Ishtar Gate of Babylon, glazed in lapis blue with golden lions, and the towering Pergamon Altar (though major renovation keeps parts closed for years). The island was devastated in the war; the Neues Museum stood as a bombed ruin for sixty years until British architect David Chipperfield's celebrated restoration deliberately preserved the scars, letting bullet holes and burnt walls tell their own story alongside the treasures.$dyk$, 52.51667, 13.40222, 80, $dyk$1830$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$mauerpark-karaoke$dyk$, $dyk$Mauerpark$dyk$, $dyk$Flea market and Sunday karaoke$dyk$, $dyk$Every Sunday, this scruffy strip of green hosts one of Europe's most joyful rituals. Mauerpark – 'Wall park' – occupies the former death strip of the Berlin Wall; the name is entirely literal, and a graffiti-covered remnant of the Wall still runs along the ridge, where anyone may legally spray paint. After reunification, the no-man's-land became a park, and the park became an institution. Sundays bring the sprawling flea market: GDR memorabilia, vinyl records, questionable furniture, street food and buskers on every corner. But the main event happens in the stone amphitheatre on the hillside: Bearpit Karaoke, started in 2009 by an Irish bike courier named Joe Hatchiban who wheeled in mobile speakers on a cargo bike. Every Sunday afternoon in season, up to two thousand spectators pack the stone steps to cheer complete strangers singing pop songs – and the crowd is famously generous, roaring loudest for the bravely off-key. Grandmothers have become momentary legends here; one regular, the octogenarian Detlef, achieved genuine fame with his rock numbers. It is Berlin distilled: history's darkest architecture recycled into cheap fun, open to absolutely everyone, slightly chaotic, and strangely moving.$dyk$, 52.5412, 13.4023, 80, $dyk$2009$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$fernsehturm-popes-revenge$dyk$, $dyk$The TV Tower$dyk$, $dyk$The Pope's Revenge over Berlin$dyk$, $dyk$At 368 metres, the Fernsehturm at Alexanderplatz is the tallest structure in Germany – and the site of the Cold War's best architectural joke. East Germany's communist leadership built the tower in the 1960s as a statement: a socialist rocket of concrete and steel, visible from every corner of both Berlins, proof of the GDR's technological might, deliberately far taller than anything in the West. Walter Ulbricht, the state's leader, personally championed it. The regime was officially atheist and had removed crosses from churches across the country. Then the tower was finished – and the sun came out. When sunlight strikes the tower's steel sphere, the reflection forms a huge, unmistakable cross of light, beaming over the atheist state's capital. Berliners immediately christened it 'the Pope's Revenge'; American President Reagan gleefully mentioned it in his 1987 Berlin speech. The regime was reportedly furious, and stories claim engineers were asked whether the effect could be removed – it couldn't, short of rebuilding the sphere. Today the tower is beloved: its rotating restaurant serves dinner at 207 metres, the observation deck offers 80-kilometre views, and the cross still appears on every sunny day, right on schedule.$dyk$, 52.5208, 13.4094, 80, $dyk$1969$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$weltzeituhr$dyk$, $dyk$The World Clock$dyk$, $dyk$Berlin's favourite meeting point$dyk$, $dyk$'Meet you at the World Clock' – few phrases are more Berlin. The ten-tonne Weltzeituhr on Alexanderplatz, a rotating cylinder displaying the time in 148 cities beneath a whirling model of the solar system, was unveiled in 1969 along with the TV Tower, part of East Germany's grand redesign of its showcase square. The designer, Erich John, was given just nine months; the clock was assembled with the help of watchmakers and engineers scrounging materials in the shortage economy of the GDR. It instantly became the city's rendezvous point – for dates, for demonstrations, for everything. There is a lovely irony in a country whose citizens were forbidden to travel installing a monument listing the time in Los Angeles, Rio and Tokyo: for many East Berliners, the clock was as close as they would ever get to those cities, a bronze atlas of forbidden dreams. Fittingly, in the autumn of 1989, some of the demonstrations that brought down the Wall gathered right here, beneath the names of cities the crowds would soon be free to visit. The clock still works, still turns, and is still where Berliners say: 'At the clock, seven o'clock.'$dyk$, 52.5213, 13.4132, 50, $dyk$1969$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$jfk-ich-bin-ein-berliner$dyk$, $dyk$Rathaus Schöneberg$dyk$, $dyk$'Ich bin ein Berliner'$dyk$, $dyk$From the steps of this town hall, on 26 June 1963, John F. Kennedy delivered four words that became one of history's most famous sentences: 'Ich bin ein Berliner.' West Berlin was then an island of freedom deep inside communist East Germany, its people never sure the West wouldn't abandon them; the Wall had gone up less than two years earlier. Before a crowd of perhaps half a million filling this square, Kennedy tore up his diplomatic script and declared that all free people, wherever they lived, were citizens of Berlin – 'and therefore, as a free man, I take pride in the words: Ich bin ein Berliner.' The crowd's roar, witnesses said, was physically frightening. Now, about the doughnut. A beloved myth claims Kennedy accidentally called himself a jam doughnut, since 'Berliner' is the name of a pastry. Linguists have thoroughly debunked this: his phrasing was grammatically correct for a metaphorical statement, the pastry is called 'Pfannkuchen' in Berlin anyway, and no one in the crowd laughed – the story only surfaced in the 1980s. Kennedy was assassinated five months later; this square was renamed John-F.-Kennedy-Platz within days. The town hall served as West Berlin's city hall throughout the Cold War, and its 'Freedom Bell', a gift from America, still rings daily at noon.$dyk$, 52.4847, 13.3444, 60, $dyk$1963$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$teufelsberg$dyk$, $dyk$Teufelsberg$dyk$, $dyk$The devil's mountain of secrets$dyk$, $dyk$The hill beneath your feet is made of a destroyed city. Teufelsberg – Devil's Mountain – is no natural rise: it is roughly 75 million cubic metres of Second World War rubble, the pulverised remains of bombed Berlin, piled up by the famous 'rubble women' and lorries over twenty years until a 120-metre artificial mountain stood over the flat city. Buried beneath it all lies an unfinished Nazi military academy that proved too solid to demolish – so Berlin simply entombed it. Then the story turns to espionage. On the summit, the American NSA built one of the Cold War's most important listening stations: the white radar domes, like giant golf balls, still crown the hill. From here, antennas eavesdropped on Soviet and East German military communications deep behind the Iron Curtain; the site was so secret that even its rumoured achievements – tapping military headquarters, tracking aircraft across Eastern Europe – remain partly classified today. Legends cling to the place: of tunnels into the buried academy, of a mobile antenna that rose from a shaft, of recordings that reached presidents within minutes. After 1990 the station was abandoned, and decades of plans – hotels, spy museums, luxury flats – all collapsed. Today it is a graffiti gallery and urban legend, with torn dome fabric flapping in the wind.$dyk$, 52.4979, 13.2413, 80, $dyk$1963$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$ghost-stations-nordbahnhof$dyk$, $dyk$Nordbahnhof Ghost Station$dyk$, $dyk$The trains that never stopped$dyk$, $dyk$For twenty-eight years, this ordinary S-Bahn station was a sealed tomb that trains rolled through without stopping. When the Wall went up in 1961, Berlin's transit map faced a problem: several Western lines passed under East Berlin territory. The Eastern regime's solution was eerie – the stations on those stretches were closed, sealed and placed under armed guard, while Western trains slowed but never stopped, their passengers peering out at dim platforms where East German border troops stood watch among decades of accumulating dust. West Berliners called them Geisterbahnhöfe – ghost stations. Nordbahnhof was one of the largest. Its street entrances were bricked up, walled into surrounding buildings; above ground, East Berliners walked past doorways that led nowhere, many of the younger ones never knowing a station lay beneath. The guards themselves were locked in – patrolling in pairs, forbidden keys to the exits, watched by their own colleagues, because the tunnels were a known escape route to the West. Several dramatic escapes happened along these tracks anyway, including guards who fled through the tunnels they were meant to seal. When the station reopened in 1990, workers found 1961 newspapers and untouched signage – a time capsule of the day the doors closed. A permanent exhibition on the ghost stations now lines its walls.$dyk$, 52.5324, 13.3886, 60, $dyk$1961$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Berlin$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$fuehrerbunker-site$dyk$, $dyk$The Führerbunker Site$dyk$, $dyk$A car park over history's abyss$dyk$, $dyk$You are standing in one of the most deliberately unremarkable places in Berlin: a residential car park, some bins, a patch of grass. Eight metres below lay the Führerbunker – the concrete warren where Adolf Hitler spent his final weeks as the Soviet army fought street by street towards this spot, and where he took his own life on 30 April 1945. In the bunker's cramped rooms, sealed under four metres of concrete, the dictator married Eva Braun hours before their joint suicide; their bodies were burned in the garden above, roughly where the parked cars now stand. For decades afterwards, the site's history was handled with intentional silence. East Germany blew up sections and flattened the ground; after reunification, Berlin resisted every impulse to mark the location, fearing it would become a shrine for neo-Nazis. The bunker's remains were left crushed and buried, its location publicly unconfirmed – a nothing-place by design. Only in 2006, during the football World Cup, was a single modest information board erected, showing the bunker's layout. The contrast is the point: a two-minute walk away rises the vast Holocaust memorial. The victims received the monument; the perpetrator got a car park.$dyk$, 52.5126, 13.3813, 40, $dyk$1945$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$anne-frank-house$dyk$, $dyk$Anne Frank House$dyk$, $dyk$The secret annexe on the canal$dyk$, $dyk$Behind the ordinary canal-house façade at Prinsengracht 263 lies the most famous hiding place in the world. From July 1942 to August 1944, eight Jewish people – among them thirteen-year-old Anne Frank and her family – lived in total silence in the 'Secret Annexe', rooms at the back of Otto Frank's business premises, concealed behind a hinged bookcase. By day they could not run water, flush the toilet or speak above a whisper, as workers who knew nothing moved about below. Anne poured everything into her red-checked diary: fear and boredom, quarrels, first love with fellow hider Peter, and a stubborn, luminous faith in people. In August 1944 they were betrayed – by whom has never been established – and deported. Anne died in Bergen-Belsen in early 1945, aged fifteen, months before liberation. Only her father Otto survived. Their helper Miep Gies had rescued the diary pages, unread, from the ransacked annexe; Otto published them in 1947. The diary has since sold over 30 million copies in some 70 languages. The rooms remain unfurnished – at Otto's request – but Anne's magazine pictures of film stars still cling to her bedroom wall.$dyk$, 52.37522, 4.88397, 50, $dyk$1942$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$royal-palace-amsterdam$dyk$, $dyk$The Royal Palace$dyk$, $dyk$A city hall fit for kings$dyk$, $dyk$The monumental building dominating Dam Square wasn't built for royalty at all – it was Amsterdam's town hall, and in its day the largest secular building in Europe, dubbed the 'Eighth Wonder of the World'. Completed in 1655 at the very peak of the Dutch Golden Age, it was a merchant republic's declaration of self-confidence: no king built this, but the citizens of a trading city richer than most kingdoms. The scale of the engineering matches the swagger – the building rests on 13,659 wooden piles driven into Amsterdam's marshy ground, a number every Dutch schoolchild once memorised. Inside, the marble Citizens' Hall represents the entire universe, with maps of the world inlaid in the floor so Amsterdammers could literally walk across their trading empire, which stretched from Manhattan (then New Amsterdam) to Indonesia. The building became a palace by force: Napoleon's brother Louis, installed as King of Holland in 1806, evicted the magistrates and moved in with his court. After Napoleon's fall, the building passed to the Dutch royal family, who still use it for state visits, receptions and royal weddings – though no royal actually lives there. When the royals are away, you can walk the universe yourself.$dyk$, 52.37312, 4.89123, 60, $dyk$1655$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$westerkerk$dyk$, $dyk$Westerkerk$dyk$, $dyk$Rembrandt's grave, Anne's bells$dyk$, $dyk$The blue imperial crown glittering atop this church tower is Amsterdam's most beloved landmark – and its 85 metres still make the Westerkerk the city's tallest church, as it has been since completion in 1631. The crown honours Maximilian I of Austria, who granted the city the right to use it in gratitude for Amsterdam's loans and loyalty – medieval sponsorship, immortalised in copper. This is the parish church of the Jordaan, the old working-class quarter, and its carillon still rings out over the canals; Anne Frank, hiding a few doors away, wrote in her diary how she loved the bells and found their chiming comforting through the nights – 'especially at night, they're like a faithful friend.' Somewhere under the church floor lies Rembrandt van Rijn. The great painter died in 1669, poor and out of fashion, and was buried here in a rented, unmarked grave; the exact spot has never been identified, and after twenty years his remains were likely cleared away, as was customary with rented graves. A memorial plaque stands in for the missing tomb. Climb the tower in summer for the classic panorama over the canal rings – guided, and not for the wobbly-kneed.$dyk$, 52.3744, 4.8831, 40, $dyk$1631$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$oude-kerk$dyk$, $dyk$Oude Kerk$dyk$, $dyk$The old church in the red light$dyk$, $dyk$Amsterdam's oldest building sits in its most notorious neighbourhood: the Oude Kerk – Old Church – consecrated around 1306, now surrounded on all sides by the red-lit windows of the De Wallen district. The juxtaposition is not new; sailors have stumbled from the port's taverns past these walls for seven centuries, and the church once did brisk business blessing their departures and burying those who didn't return. The entire floor is made of gravestones – some 2,500 of them, covering around 10,000 burials, because the church was built on a cemetery and simply kept burying downwards. Amsterdammers of rank paid for a spot; the most famous resident is Saskia van Uylenburgh, Rembrandt's beloved wife, whose grave the painter had to sell in his later poverty. Above, the wooden vault – the largest medieval wooden ceiling in Europe – still shows 15th-century paintings, and the great Vater-Müller organ is one of the finest in the country. Look for the small carved misericords under the choir seats, where medieval woodcarvers hid earthy jokes and proverbs. Today the church doubles as a contemporary art space, hosting installations that make the old walls argue with the new world – very Amsterdam.$dyk$, 52.37418, 4.89808, 50, $dyk$1306$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$night-watch-rijksmuseum$dyk$, $dyk$The Night Watch, Rijksmuseum$dyk$, $dyk$Rembrandt's restless masterpiece$dyk$, $dyk$At the heart of the Rijksmuseum, at the end of a cathedral-like Gallery of Honour, hangs the painting the entire building was designed around: Rembrandt's Night Watch of 1642. It shouldn't be exciting – it's a group portrait of a civic militia company, the 17th-century equivalent of a corporate team photo. But where colleagues painted stiff rows of faces, Rembrandt detonated the genre: Captain Frans Banninck Cocq strides forward mid-order, muskets are loaded, a drum beats, a dog barks, and a mysterious golden girl with a chicken at her belt glows in the chaos. The militiamen who each paid for a portrait got a thriller instead; some, faces half-hidden, reportedly grumbled. The painting's later life is equally dramatic. It isn't even a night scene – darkened varnish created the name; cleaning revealed daylight. In 1715 the canvas was brutally trimmed on all sides to fit a wall in the town hall, losing two figures forever. It has survived three attacks: slashed with a shoemaker's knife in 1911, gravely slashed again in 1975, and sprayed with acid in 1990. Since 2019 the museum has been restoring it inside a glass chamber, live in front of visitors – 'Operation Night Watch', the most watched restoration in art history.$dyk$, 52.36, 4.88539, 70, $dyk$1642$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$rembrandt-house$dyk$, $dyk$The Rembrandt House$dyk$, $dyk$Where the master rose and fell$dyk$, $dyk$In 1639, at the height of his fame, Rembrandt van Rijn bought this grand house on what was then the edge of Amsterdam's Jewish quarter – and it became both his glory and his ruin. Here he painted The Night Watch, ran a busy studio full of pupils, taught, dealt in art, and collected obsessively: weapons, busts, exotic shells, stuffed animals, costumes and prints filled his 'art room' like a private museum, props for paintings and objects of pure delight. Here too his life unravelled. His wife Saskia died in 1640, a year after they moved in; three of their four children died in infancy. Rembrandt, catastrophic with money, had bought the house with an enormous loan he never mastered. In 1656 he was declared insolvent, and the meticulous bankruptcy inventory of his possessions – 363 items – became an accidental gift to history: because of it, we know exactly what he owned and how he lived. The house was sold, and the ageing master moved to cheaper rooms in the Jordaan. Today the rebuilt interior follows that inventory room by room, with his etching press running daily demonstrations. You are, quite literally, walking through a bankruptcy filing – the most evocative one in art history.$dyk$, 52.36938, 4.90114, 40, $dyk$1639$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$vondelpark$dyk$, $dyk$Vondelpark$dyk$, $dyk$Amsterdam's living room in the open air$dyk$, $dyk$When wealthy Amsterdammers opened this 'Riding and Strolling Park' in 1865, they could hardly have imagined it hosting sleeping hippies by the thousand – but more on that shortly. The 47-hectare park, named after the 17th-century poet Joost van den Vondel, was laid out in fashionable English landscape style: winding paths, ponds, rose gardens and theatrical clumps of trees, all resting – like everything here – on soft peat, which is why the park sinks slightly and endlessly, requiring perpetual maintenance. Around ten million visits a year make it one of Europe's most intensively loved parks. Its wildest chapter came in the early 1970s, when Amsterdam's tolerant magistrates allowed young travellers to sleep in the park; the 'Vondelpark sleepers' became a hippie-era legend, with up to a hundred thousand young people from across the world camping here each summer until the practice ended in 1975. The spirit lingers: free concerts at the open-air theatre each summer, ubiquitous picnics, and a general conviction that the park belongs to everyone. Note the roses in the rose garden – 70-plus varieties – and Picasso's 'The Fish', a five-metre concrete sculpture the artist donated for the park's centenary in 1965.$dyk$, 52.3579, 4.8685, 80, $dyk$1865$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$van-gogh-museum$dyk$, $dyk$Van Gogh Museum$dyk$, $dyk$The world's largest Van Gogh collection$dyk$, $dyk$This museum owes its existence to a sister-in-law's stubbornness. When Vincent van Gogh died in 1890, aged 37, he had sold barely a painting; his brother Theo, who had supported him all his life, died just six months later. Everything – hundreds of paintings and drawings nobody wanted – passed to Theo's young widow, Johanna van Gogh-Bonger. Instead of quietly selling off the awkward inheritance, Johanna dedicated her life to it: she organised exhibitions, lent works strategically, and published Vincent's letters to Theo, correctly sensing that the tragic, luminous life story would open the world's eyes to the art. It worked beyond all imagining. The collection stayed in the family and became this museum in 1973: over 200 paintings, 500 drawings and more than 800 letters – the largest Van Gogh collection in the world. The galleries walk you through the whole arc: the dark Potato Eaters of his Dutch years, the explosion of colour in Paris and Arles, the Sunflowers, the ominous Wheatfield with Crows painted weeks before his death. The letters hang beside the canvases, and it's the combination that devastates: a man describing exactly what he's attempting, decade after decade, to an audience of one loyal brother.$dyk$, 52.3585, 4.88103, 60, $dyk$1973$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$bloemenmarkt$dyk$, $dyk$Bloemenmarkt$dyk$, $dyk$The floating flower market$dyk$, $dyk$The flower stalls lining the Singel canal since 1862 form the world's only floating flower market – though you have to peek behind the shops to see it: the stalls stand on houseboats moored to the quay, a legacy of the days when growers sailed in daily from the countryside and sold their blooms straight from the barge. Flowers are, of course, the Netherlands' national obsession, and the market is a year-round blaze of tulips, narcissi, geraniums and bulbs by the sackful, alongside a great deal of cheerful kitsch – wooden tulips, cannabis-leaf lollipops and clog-shaped everything. The tulip itself carries Amsterdam's maddest story: in the 1630s, 'tulip mania' gripped the city, and speculation drove single rare bulbs to the price of a canal house – the bulb trade collapsing spectacularly in February 1637, history's most famous market bubble. The most coveted variety, the flame-striped Semper Augustus, owed its beauty to a virus, unknown at the time, that 'broke' the colour into streaks. Today a sack of bulbs costs a few euros; check they carry the export certificate if you're taking them home. Winter visitors get their own spectacle, when the market glows during the season's light festival.$dyk$, 52.3668, 4.8915, 60, $dyk$1862$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$narrowest-house-amsterdam$dyk$, $dyk$The Narrowest House$dyk$, $dyk$A house the width of a door$dyk$, $dyk$Squeeze past Oude Hoogstraat 22 and you might miss an entire building: at just 2.02 metres wide, this is often called the narrowest house in Amsterdam – barely wider than its own front door, though it stretches surprisingly deep behind the façade, like a corridor that decided to become a home. Amsterdam's skinny houses are no accident. In the Golden Age, property taxes were levied largely on canal frontage – the width of your house facing the street – so canny merchants built narrow and deep, saving on tax while keeping warehouse capacity. The whole city is shaped by this fiscal dodge: tall, slim houses with hoisting beams jutting from their gables, because the staircases inside are far too narrow for furniture, which is still hauled up and swung in through the windows today. Several houses compete for the 'narrowest' crown depending on how you measure: the house at Singel 7 has a rear façade barely a metre wide (though it widens greedily behind), while Kloveniersburgwal 26, the 'Kleine Trippenhuis', is 2.44 metres wide and stands, deliciously, opposite the vast mansion of the Trip brothers – legend claims their coachman sighed he'd be happy with a house as wide as his masters' front door, and was heard.$dyk$, 52.3705, 4.8983, 40, $dyk$18th century$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$de-poezenboot$dyk$, $dyk$De Poezenboot$dyk$, $dyk$The cat boat of Amsterdam$dyk$, $dyk$Moored on the Singel canal floats one of the world's most peculiar animal shelters: De Poezenboot, the Cat Boat – a houseboat that is home to a shifting crew of rescued cats. The story began in 1966, when a woman named Henriette van Weelde took pity on a stray cat family sheltering under a tree opposite her canal house. She took them in. Then more came, as cats do, and word spread that the lady on the Singel helped strays; soon her house and garden overflowed. In 1968 she bought an old Dutch sailing barge and converted it into a feline sanctuary – logical enough in a city where houses float. A second boat followed in 1971, and 'the cat lady of the Singel' became a city institution. Today the boat is a registered charity with volunteers, veterinary care and an adoption programme; it shelters dozens of cats at a time, the permanent residents being those too wild or too old for adoption, who live out their days watching the canal traffic slide by. Visitors are welcome during opening hours, and the boat has become a beloved offbeat attraction – reviewers note, accurately, that it is a boat full of cats, and that this is exactly enough.$dyk$, 52.3781, 4.8907, 40, $dyk$1968$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$dancing-houses-damrak$dyk$, $dyk$The Dancing Houses$dyk$, $dyk$Why Amsterdam's houses lean$dyk$, $dyk$Look across the water at the row of canal houses on the Damrak – tilting, leaning against each other, none quite vertical – and you're looking at Amsterdam's most photographed architectural quirk: the 'dancing houses'. The lean is partly deliberate and partly the swamp fighting back. Amsterdam is built on metres of soft peat and clay; every building stands on wooden piles driven down to a firm sand layer, and the old joke says the city is 'built on herring bones'. When piles rot, snap or settle unevenly – wooden piles survive beautifully underwater but decay the moment air reaches them – houses begin to tip and slump against their neighbours, gaining what locals fondly call their 'dancing' posture. But look at the fronts of many houses across the city and you'll see they also lean forward on purpose. Builders gave façades a deliberate tilt ('op de vlucht' – on the run so goods hoisted to the attic on the gable beam wouldn't scrape or smash against the frontage, and rain would drip clear of the brick. Regulations even specified permissible lean. The result is a city that stands slightly tipsy by design and slightly tipsier by subsidence – and has decided, sensibly, to find it charming.$dyk$, 52.3756, 4.8968, 50, $dyk$17th century$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$begijnhof-grave-in-the-gutter$dyk$, $dyk$The Begijnhof$dyk$, $dyk$The nun buried in the gutter$dyk$, $dyk$Step through an unassuming door off the busy Spui square and the city falls silent: you're in the Begijnhof, a hidden medieval courtyard of gabled houses around a green, founded in the 14th century for the Beguines – devout Catholic women who lived like nuns but took no permanent vows, free to leave and marry. The last Beguine died in 1971, ending six centuries of continuous sisterhood; the houses are still reserved for single women today. The courtyard holds Amsterdam's oldest surviving house, the wooden Houten Huys from around 1530, and a 'hidden church' where Catholics worshipped in secret after the Protestant takeover of 1578 – officially forbidden, unofficially tolerated, very Amsterdam. But the courtyard's strangest resident lies in the pavement. Cornelia Arens, a Beguine who died in 1654, was so ashamed of her family's conversion to Protestantism that she asked to be buried not in the chapel but in the gutter – humble ground for what she saw as the family's sin. The legend insists her coffin was placed in the chapel anyway, and reappeared in the gutter the next morning – twice – until the sisters gave in. A grave slab by the path marks where she finally rested, and flowers still appear on it, centuries later.$dyk$, 52.3689, 4.8899, 50, $dyk$1654$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$house-with-the-heads$dyk$, $dyk$The House with the Heads$dyk$, $dyk$Six heads and a headless legend$dyk$, $dyk$The grand Renaissance canal house at Keizersgracht 123, built in 1622, wears six sculpted heads across its façade – and a legend far better than the truth. Officially, the heads represent six classical gods: Apollo, Ceres, Mars, Minerva, Bacchus and Diana, a fashionable display of learning for the wealthy merchant family who built the house. But Amsterdammers have always preferred the other version. In that story, six burglars tried to break into the house's cellar one night while only the kitchen maid was home. The maid, hearing noises, took up a sword – and as each burglar stuck his head through the cellar hatch in turn, she calmly chopped it off. Six burglars, six heads. The heads on the façade, says the legend, were carved to commemorate her deed, and in the most romantic version the impressed master of the house married her. Tour guides have embroidered the tale for centuries – sometimes the sixth burglar begs for mercy and marries her instead. The house's documented history is rich enough: it hosted one of Amsterdam's great Golden Age merchant dynasties, later a famous library of hermetic and alchemical texts, adding a whiff of the occult to a façade already crowned with six severed heads.$dyk$, 52.3765, 4.8876, 40, $dyk$1622$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Amsterdam$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$miracle-of-amsterdam$dyk$, $dyk$The Miracle of Amsterdam$dyk$, $dyk$The host that would not burn$dyk$, $dyk$Amsterdam owes its rise, in part, to a fireplace that failed to do its job. In March 1345, in a house on the Kalverstraat, a dying man received the last sacraments and vomited up the communion wafer. Following custom, the attending woman threw the sick-room waste, wafer included, into the fire. The next morning, the story goes, the host lay unburnt and glowing among the embers; a servant reached in and lifted it out with her bare hand, unharmed. The wafer was carried to the parish church – and returned, by itself, to the house. Twice. The Church declared a miracle, a chapel called the Holy Stead rose on the site, and Amsterdam – until then a modest fishing town – became one of northern Europe's great pilgrimage destinations, drawing emperors and crowds whose spending helped fund the city's growth. Even Maximilian of Austria came as a pilgrim, and his gratitude gave the city the crown on the Westerkerk tower. The Protestant takeover ended official Catholic processions, but the miracle refused to die out: every March since 1881, thousands of Catholics walk the silent night-time 'Stille Omgang' through the city centre, retracing the old pilgrims' route without banners or song – a procession disguised as a stroll, still circling a fire that went out seven centuries ago.$dyk$, 52.3694, 4.8929, 40, $dyk$1345$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$schoenbrunn-palace$dyk$, $dyk$Schönbrunn Palace$dyk$, $dyk$The Habsburgs' summer dream$dyk$, $dyk$The golden-yellow palace stretching before you was the Habsburgs' answer to Versailles – 1,441 rooms of imperial summer splendour, rebuilt to its present glory from 1743 under Empress Maria Theresa, the formidable mother of sixteen children who ran an empire between births. Her daughter Marie Antoinette played in these gardens before her appointment with the French guillotine. In the Hall of Mirrors, a six-year-old prodigy named Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart performed for the Empress in 1762 – and, according to beloved family lore, jumped into her lap, kissed her, and proposed marriage to the young archduchess Marie Antoinette after she helped him up from the polished floor. Napoleon used the palace as his headquarters twice; his only legitimate son, given the title Duke of Reichstadt, lived and died young here, a gilded prisoner. The palace's last great resident, Emperor Franz Joseph, was born here in 1830 and died here in 1916, having ruled for 68 years; his spartan iron bed still contrasts strangely with the rococo gold around it. The gardens alone deserve hours: the Gloriette on its hill, the maze, and the world's oldest zoo, founded 1752.$dyk$, 48.1849, 16.3122, 80, $dyk$1743$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$st-stephens-cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$St Stephen's Cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Vienna's soul in stone and tile$dyk$, $dyk$The Viennese call it simply 'Steffl' – little Stephen – with the affection reserved for family. St Stephen's Cathedral has stood at the city's heart since the 12th century, its south tower rising 136 metres, for centuries the tallest thing anyone in Vienna had ever seen; watchmen lived in its tip until 1955, scanning for fires. The glorious roof mosaic of 230,000 glazed tiles displays the double-headed imperial eagle, and beneath the floor the catacombs hold thousands of Viennese – including bronze urns containing the entrails of Habsburg rulers, since the dynasty famously distributed its dead across three churches: hearts to the Augustinians, innards here, bodies to the Capuchin crypt. Mozart was married in this cathedral in 1782 and his funeral rites were read here in 1791. The building's darkest hour came in April 1945, in the war's final days, when sparks from burning buildings ignited the roof; the great bell, the Pummerin – cast originally from captured Turkish cannons after the 1683 siege – crashed down and shattered. Its recast successor, Austria's largest bell, now rings only on special occasions, most famously at New Year, when all Austria toasts to its toll on live television.$dyk$, 48.20849, 16.3732, 60, $dyk$1160$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$hofburg$dyk$, $dyk$The Hofburg$dyk$, $dyk$Six centuries of imperial power$dyk$, $dyk$This immense palace complex – 18 wings, 19 courtyards, some 2,600 rooms – was the Habsburgs' seat of power for over six centuries, the command centre of an empire that at its height stretched from Spain to Hungary and across the oceans. Each era added architecture: medieval core, Renaissance courtyards, baroque state halls, right up to the grandiose New Castle wing finished just as the empire collapsed in 1918 – imperial timing at its finest. Today the Hofburg contains the Austrian president's offices, the imperial apartments of Franz Joseph and his wife Elisabeth – the legendary Sisi, whose beauty regime, gymnastics equipment and melancholy poetry fill the museum devoted to her – and the imperial treasury, home to the thousand-year-old crown of the Holy Roman Empire and, among the relics, the Holy Lance, claimed to have pierced Christ's side. Here too are the Spanish Riding School, where white Lipizzaner stallions perform baroque ballet under crystal chandeliers, and the chapel where the Vienna Boys' Choir sings Sunday mass, both institutions running for centuries. History's echoes are not all gentle: from the New Castle's balcony, Hitler proclaimed Austria's annexation to a vast crowd on Heldenplatz in 1938 – a moment Austria has debated ever since.$dyk$, 48.2077, 16.3653, 80, $dyk$13th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$klimt-kiss-belvedere$dyk$, $dyk$The Kiss, Belvedere Palace$dyk$, $dyk$Gold, love and scandal$dyk$, $dyk$Inside the baroque Belvedere palace hangs Austria's most beloved treasure: Gustav Klimt's The Kiss, painted 1907–1908 at the height of his 'golden period'. A couple kneels on a flower-strewn cliff edge, wrapped in robes of ornament and genuine gold leaf, the world dissolved into shimmer around them – tenderness armoured in Byzantine gold. Klimt, son of a gold engraver, knew his material intimately. Vienna around 1900 was scandalised and enthralled by him: his earlier ceiling paintings for the university were denounced as pornographic, prompting Klimt to declare he would never again accept a public commission – 'enough of censorship... I want to get away.' The Kiss, though, was an instant triumph: the Austrian state bought it straight from the easel, before it was even finished, for a record price. The identity of the models remains one of art's favourite guessing games – most bets are on Klimt himself and Emilie Flöge, the fashion designer who was his lifelong companion, though the painter, who never married and fathered at least fourteen children by various muses, left no answer. He worked in a floor-length blue smock with, reportedly, nothing underneath. The Belvedere itself, summer palace of Prince Eugene of Savoy, is worth the visit alone – and its upper terrace frames the classic postcard view of Vienna.$dyk$, 48.1914, 16.3808, 70, $dyk$1908$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$mozarthaus$dyk$, $dyk$Mozarthaus Vienna$dyk$, $dyk$Where Figaro was born$dyk$, $dyk$In the elegant apartment behind this façade on Domgasse, a few steps from the cathedral, lived history's most astonishing musical mind at the peak of his glory. Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart rented the first floor here from 1784 to 1787 – his grandest and most expensive Viennese address, and the only one of his dozen city apartments to survive. These were his golden years: here he composed The Marriage of Figaro, which gave the house its nickname 'Figarohaus', wrote some of his greatest piano concertos, hosted billiard games (he was an enthusiastic and reportedly average player), kept a starling that could whistle a theme from his piano concerto – he bought the bird after hearing it in a shop and gave it a mock-solemn funeral when it died – and received a visit from Joseph Haydn, who told Mozart's father Leopold: 'Your son is the greatest composer known to me in person or by name.' Mozart lived extravagantly, earning fortunes and spending them faster – the fine apartment, a horse, servants, gambling. Within four years of leaving this address he would be dead at 35, buried in a common grave whose exact location remains unknown. The house lets you stand in the rooms where, for a few years, everything was still golden.$dyk$, 48.208, 16.3756, 40, $dyk$1784$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$maria-theresa-monument$dyk$, $dyk$Maria Theresa Monument$dyk$, $dyk$The empress between the museums$dyk$, $dyk$Enthroned in bronze between Vienna's twin museum palaces sits the only woman ever to rule the Habsburg empire – and one of the most remarkable monarchs Europe produced. Maria Theresa inherited the throne in 1740 at 23, when European powers assumed the young woman's realm could simply be carved up; she spent the next forty years proving them expensively wrong, reforming the army, schools, and administration while giving birth to sixteen children, whom she strategically married into every court on the continent – earning the nickname 'mother-in-law of Europe'. Her daughter Marie Antoinette went to France, and to the guillotine. The empress worked through her pregnancies, reviewed troops, and ran cabinet meetings; when the Hungarian nobility hesitated to support her, she appeared before them with her infant son in her arms and won them over in a scene that became legend. The 1888 monument surrounds her with her generals, advisers and the geniuses of her age – look for the boy Mozart tucked among the figures. She sits holding the Pragmatic Sanction, the document that made a woman's rule possible. Vienna's wits note that even in bronze, she occupies the city's most commanding seat, between the treasures of art and nature her dynasty collected.$dyk$, 48.2053, 16.3611, 50, $dyk$1888$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$prater-riesenrad$dyk$, $dyk$The Prater Giant Ferris Wheel$dyk$, $dyk$Vienna's slow-motion skyline ride$dyk$, $dyk$Turning serenely above the old imperial hunting grounds since 1897, the Riesenrad is one of the world's oldest operating Ferris wheels – and quite possibly its most cinematic. It was built by English engineers to celebrate Emperor Franz Joseph's golden jubilee, one of a family of great wheels erected around the world in those years; the Vienna wheel is the survivor, still circling at a stately 75 centimetres per second, slow enough for conversation, proposals and espionage. Its red wooden cabins – fifteen today, half the original thirty, which were not replaced after wartime fire and rebuilding – have hosted everything from candlelit dinners to weddings. The espionage is not a joke: in the 1949 film noir The Third Man, Orson Welles' black-marketeer Harry Lime delivers his famous cynical monologue in cabin conversation high above the ruined post-war city, looking down at the people below as 'dots'. The film made the wheel a world icon; the James Bond adventure The Living Daylights added a spy kiss in 1987. Beneath it spreads the Prater itself: the vast public park – imperial hunting reserve until Joseph II opened it to everyone in 1766 – with its beloved, gloriously old-fashioned amusement park, chestnut avenues and beer gardens.$dyk$, 48.2166, 16.3958, 70, $dyk$1897$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$johann-strauss-monument$dyk$, $dyk$The Johann Strauss Monument$dyk$, $dyk$The golden waltz king$dyk$, $dyk$The gilded violinist playing eternally under his marble arch in the Stadtpark is Vienna's most photographed statue – Johann Strauss the Younger, the Waltz King, who set the 19th century dancing. Strauss wrote nearly 500 works, among them The Blue Danube, Austria's unofficial anthem, and operettas like Die Fledermaus; his concerts caused hysteria decades before anyone coined 'Beatlemania', and on his American tour he conducted monster concerts with thousands of performers. His father, Johann senior, was himself Vienna's waltz master and fiercely opposed his son's musical career – the boy studied violin in secret, and when he debuted at 18, rival factions of father-fans and son-fans turned concert halls into battlegrounds. The son won, definitively: when father died, Johann junior merged both orchestras and toured Europe as an industry of one, so overworked he sometimes composed on his shirt cuffs. The monument, unveiled in 1921 amid the grey hunger of post-imperial Vienna, gave the city back its golden self-image – literally: the bronze is fully gilded, and when the gilding was renewed, Vienna celebrated. Around it, the Stadtpark plays its role as the city's melodic salon; the Kursalon pavilion nearby, where Strauss himself performed, still hosts nightly waltz concerts for visitors.$dyk$, 48.2044, 16.3799, 50, $dyk$1921$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$volksgarten$dyk$, $dyk$The Volksgarten$dyk$, $dyk$Roses, Sisi and quiet splendour$dyk$, $dyk$Squeezed between the Hofburg and the Ringstrasse lies Vienna's most elegant pocket of green – the Volksgarten, the 'People's Garden', opened in 1823 as the first Viennese park explicitly created for the public rather than the court. It owes its existence to Napoleon, in a backhanded way: his troops blew up the city bastions here in 1809, and rather than rebuild the fortifications, Vienna eventually planted a garden over the rubble. At its centre stands a full-scale replica of the Temple of Theseus in Athens, built to house a Canova sculpture and now hosting exhibitions; around it spreads the garden's glory – more than 3,000 rose bushes of hundreds of varieties, each labelled, many 'adopted' by sponsors whose names hang on small plaques. In June the scent is nearly architectural. Tucked in a quiet corner sits the melancholy memorial to Empress Elisabeth – Sisi – murdered by an anarchist in Geneva in 1898: a seated marble figure gazing past the flowerbeds, mourning built as landscape. The park has long been Vienna's genteel promenade; Theodor Herzl thought here, and the café in the old glasshouse still serves its patrons beneath the chestnuts as the Burgtheater's façade watches over the lawns.$dyk$, 48.2083, 16.3623, 70, $dyk$1823$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$hundertwasserhaus$dyk$, $dyk$Hundertwasserhaus$dyk$, $dyk$The house with no straight lines$dyk$, $dyk$The building before you looks like a child's drawing come to life – and that was precisely the intention. The artist Friedensreich Hundertwasser despised what he called the 'godless and immoral' straight line, declaring it the tool of tyranny in architecture; when Vienna gave him a social housing block to design, completed in 1985, he answered with this: a rolling, tilting facade in patches of colour, columns like ceramic candy, golden onion domes, floors inside that undulate like forest paths – 'an uneven floor is a melody to the feet', he insisted – and over 200 trees and shrubs growing from balconies and rooftops. Some windows even have 'tree tenants': trees leaning out where a person might. Hundertwasser also proclaimed 'window rights': every resident, he said, should be free to lean out and repaint the wall around their own window as far as their arm can reach. He took no fee for the design, saying he was happy to have prevented something ugly from being built there instead. The apartments remain ordinary rented social housing – you can't go in – but the shopping arcade opposite, also by the artist, offers the full wavy-floor experience. The residents' quiet daily life inside a global tourist magnet is its own Viennese comedy.$dyk$, 48.2072, 16.3944, 50, $dyk$1985$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$ankeruhr$dyk$, $dyk$The Anker Clock$dyk$, $dyk$Twelve figures parade at noon$dyk$, $dyk$Spanning the gap between two buildings on Hoher Markt – Vienna's oldest square, once the Roman forum of Vindobona – stretches one of Europe's loveliest public clocks. The Anker Clock, an Art Nouveau jewel of copper, gold and mosaic, was created between 1911 and 1914 by the painter Franz Matsch as an advertisement, essentially: the Anker insurance company commissioned it to bridge its two office buildings, with a message about time, life and the wisdom of insurance woven into its symbolism. Every hour, a gilded historical figure glides across the clock face: Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius, who died in Vindobona; Charlemagne; Duke Rudolf the Founder; Maria Theresa with her husband; Joseph Haydn, and more – twelve figures spanning Vienna's whole history. The full spectacle comes at noon, when all twelve parade past in sequence to a program of organ music, taking about fifteen minutes while a crowd gathers below with cameras raised. Beneath the clock, a plaque notes the figures' identities for the puzzled. The clock survived the Second World War with only moderate damage while the square around it burned – the insurance company's masterpiece, insured by luck. Come just before twelve; the daily parade has been running for over a century.$dyk$, 48.2111, 16.3731, 40, $dyk$1914$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$kapuzinergruft-habsburg-hearts$dyk$, $dyk$The Capuchin Crypt$dyk$, $dyk$Where emperors knock to enter$dyk$, $dyk$Beneath a deliberately modest monastery church rest 150 Habsburgs in the strangest imperial cemetery in Europe. Since 1633, the dynasty that ruled half the world buried its dead here – but not all of each one. Following a macabre tradition, many Habsburgs were divided in death like relics: bodies to this Capuchin Crypt, hearts in silver urns to the Augustinian Church, entrails to the catacombs of St Stephen's Cathedral. Three churches, one emperor each. The sarcophagi tell the dynasty's story in metal: Maria Theresa shares with her husband a rococo double sarcophagus the size of a room, upon which their sculpted figures recline gazing at each other as an angel wakes them – while their modest son Joseph II lies beside them in a plain copper box, minimalist even in death. The crypt's most touching legend is the admission ritual, still performed at Habsburg funerals, most recently for Otto von Habsburg in 2011: the procession knocks at the door, and a friar asks 'Who desires entry?' The herald lists the deceased's grand titles – emperor, king, archduke – and the friar replies 'We do not know him.' Titles again, shorter; again, 'We do not know him.' Only when the answer is 'a mortal, sinful human being' does the door open.$dyk$, 48.2056, 16.3701, 40, $dyk$1633$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$basilisk-schoenlaterngasse$dyk$, $dyk$The Basilisk House$dyk$, $dyk$The monster in the well$dyk$, $dyk$In the crooked, lantern-lit Schönlaterngasse – the 'beautiful lantern lane' – one house wears a monster on its façade. The relief and fresco commemorate Vienna's favourite medieval horror story, set, with bureaucratic precision, in the year 1212. That summer, the legend runs, a foul stench rose from the house's well, and a servant girl sent to investigate fainted at what she saw: at the bottom sat a basilisk – a nightmare creature hatched, as everyone then knew, from a rooster's egg brooded by a toad, crowned like a cock, tailed like a serpent, with breath of poison and a gaze that killed instantly. The neighbourhood panicked; no one could approach the well and live. Then a young baker's apprentice, in love with his master's daughter and promised her hand if he proved his courage, remembered his natural history: the basilisk cannot survive its own reflection. He descended the well backwards, carrying a polished mirror, and held it before the creature – which saw itself, and in its horror burst, or in gentler versions turned to stone. The stone in the courtyard niche, of course, is said to be the petrified basilisk itself; sober geologists note it is a weathered sandstone lump, which has never dented the story. The apprentice got the girl. The well was filled.$dyk$, 48.2089, 16.3776, 40, $dyk$1212$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$stock-im-eisen$dyk$, $dyk$The Stock im Eisen$dyk$, $dyk$The nail-studded tree of pacts$dyk$, $dyk$Set into a corner niche where the Graben meets Kärntner Strasse, behind protective glass, stands one of Vienna's strangest relics: a section of medieval tree trunk so densely hammered with hundreds of nails that iron has almost replaced wood. The Stock im Eisen – 'staff in iron' – is first mentioned in 1533, and the tree itself, a spruce, was alive in the 15th century; dendrochronology dates its felling to around 1440. Why centuries of people nailed iron into it is genuinely uncertain, which has left glorious room for legend. The most famous stars, inevitably, the Devil: a locksmith's apprentice, desperate to win a wager or a girl, sold his soul in exchange for making an unpickable lock for the iron band around the trunk – or, in variants, for the skill to forge nails no one else could. The Devil kept his end; the boy tried to cheat his, and was carried off through a window, which is why, old Viennese would tell you, the house nearby always kept one window walled shut. More sober historians suggest travelling journeymen hammered in nails for luck upon arriving in Vienna – a medieval version of the tourist padlock. The lock on the iron band, naturally, is said to be unopenable. No one has publicly tried in centuries.$dyk$, 48.2079, 16.3711, 40, $dyk$1440$dyk$, true, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Vienna$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$der-liebe-augustin-griechenbeisl$dyk$, $dyk$Griechenbeisl & Dear Augustin$dyk$, $dyk$The drunk who out-sang the plague$dyk$, $dyk$Vienna's oldest inn, the Griechenbeisl – serving since the 15th century, with Beethoven, Schubert, Mark Twain and Johann Strauss in its guestbook – guards the city's most cheerful horror story. In the plague year 1679, when corpse carts rattled nightly through the streets, there lived a bagpiper and ballad singer called Augustin, famous for being drunk more reliably than the church bells rang. One night, sodden with wine, Augustin collapsed in the gutter, and the corpse collectors, mistaking him for another victim, tossed him – bagpipes and all – onto their cart and tipped him into a mass plague pit outside the walls. Augustin woke at dawn among the dead, unbothered and mildly curious, climbed out unable to say quite how, and – in the best versions – piped a tune from the pit's edge until rescuers arrived. He then returned to the taverns, where he drank on for years and died, pointedly, of alcohol rather than plague. The Viennese adored him: a man who spent a night in a plague pit and simply carried on became the city's mascot of indestructible good humour, immortalised in the song 'O du lieber Augustin'. A figure of Augustin waves from the Griechenbeisl's facade, and inside, one vaulted room bears centuries of famous guests' signatures on its ceiling.$dyk$, 48.21146, 16.3769, 40, $dyk$1679$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$charles-bridge$dyk$, $dyk$Charles Bridge$dyk$, $dyk$The stone bridge of kings and saints$dyk$, $dyk$You are crossing one of the most beautiful bridges in the world – and one founded on astrology. Emperor Charles IV, a devoted numerologist, laid the first stone of his bridge in 1357, on the 9th of July at 5:31 in the morning, so the date would form a numerical palindrome: 1357 9/7 5:31. The bridge took nearly half a century to build, its sandstone piers – legend insists, and analysis has partly confirmed – mortared with eggs, which villages across Bohemia dutifully carted to Prague; one village, missing the point, sent theirs hard-boiled. For over 400 years this was Prague's only bridge, carrying coronation processions, armies and trade between the Old Town and the Castle. The thirty baroque statues lining the parapets – most now replicas, the originals safe in museums – were added around 1700, turning the crossing into an open-air gallery of saints. The most famous marks the spot where St John of Nepomuk was thrown into the Vltava in 1393, allegedly for refusing to reveal the queen's confession to her jealous husband. Touch the polished bronze relief at his statue and you're performing Prague's most popular ritual: it grants luck and a promised return to the city. Come at dawn, when the crowds are gone and the bridge belongs to swans and photographers.$dyk$, 50.0865, 14.4114, 80, $dyk$1357$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$prague-castle$dyk$, $dyk$Prague Castle$dyk$, $dyk$The largest castle complex on Earth$dyk$, $dyk$The hilltop acropolis above the river holds a world record: Prague Castle, founded around the year 880, is by Guinness reckoning the largest ancient castle complex on Earth – nearly 70,000 square metres of palaces, churches, towers and gardens accumulated over eleven centuries. Every power that ruled the Czech lands ruled from here: Bohemian dukes and kings, Holy Roman Emperors – including Charles IV, who made Prague an imperial capital, and the eccentric Rudolf II, who filled the castle with alchemists, astronomers and wonder-cabinets – then Habsburgs, Nazi occupiers, Communist presidents, and today's Czech head of state, whose flag flies when he is home. At its heart soars St Vitus Cathedral, six centuries in the building, guarding the Bohemian crown jewels behind a door with seven locks whose seven keys are held by seven different officials, from the president down. The castle gave the world a word, too: 'defenestration'. In 1618, Protestant nobles hurled two Catholic imperial governors and their secretary from a castle window – all three survived the 17-metre drop, landing, depending on your source, in a providential dung heap or by the intervention of angels – and the incident ignited the Thirty Years' War, which devastated Europe.$dyk$, 50.0909, 14.4005, 80, $dyk$880$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$golden-lane$dyk$, $dyk$Golden Lane$dyk$, $dyk$Tiny houses, alchemists and Kafka$dyk$, $dyk$Tucked inside the castle walls runs a miniature street from a fairy tale: Golden Lane, a row of tiny, crooked, brightly painted houses built into the arches of the fortification in the late 16th century. The first residents were castle marksmen – the guards of Rudolf II – who squeezed homes into the arcades; goldsmiths followed, giving the lane its name. The grander legend claims Rudolf's alchemists lived here, toiling to transmute lead into gold for the emperor who employed more occultists than any ruler in Europe. Historians place the actual alchemists' workshops elsewhere in the castle, but the lane has never surrendered the story – nor should it, since the truth of Rudolf's Prague was strange enough: his court hosted the astronomers Tycho Brahe and Johannes Kepler, the English magus John Dee and the alchemist Edward Kelley, making the castle Europe's capital of the weird sciences. The lane's most famous later resident was Franz Kafka, who in 1916–17 borrowed his sister's rented house at No. 22 – the little blue one – as a quiet place to write; several stories from the collection 'A Country Doctor' were written in that dollhouse room. The houses, inhabited until the Second World War, now hold period interiors, armour displays and, inevitably, souvenir shops.$dyk$, 50.0919, 14.4036, 50, $dyk$16th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tyn-church$dyk$, $dyk$Church of Our Lady before Týn$dyk$, $dyk$Gothic towers over the Old Town$dyk$, $dyk$The twin spires bristling over the Old Town Square, each sprouting a forest of smaller pinnacles, are Prague's most fairy-tale silhouette – Disney castles owe them a debt. The Church of Our Lady before Týn rose from 1365 as the Old Town's main church, its name referring to the fortified merchants' courtyard – the Týn – behind it. Look closely at the towers and you'll see they are not identical: one is visibly broader than the other, which guides explain, with a wink, as depicting the masculine and feminine sides of the world – medieval builders were rarely so poetic; asymmetry was simply how construction in stages worked out. The church spent two centuries as the flagship of the Hussites, the Czech proto-Protestant movement, its gable crowned by a golden chalice, their symbol; when the Catholic Habsburgs won, the chalice was melted down – into a halo for a statue of the Madonna, a conversion of symbols as pointed as any sermon. Inside lies the marble tomb of Tycho Brahe, Rudolf II's brilliant Danish court astronomer, complete with his famous metal nose – he lost the original in a duel over a mathematical formula. Legend claims he died of a burst bladder from politeness at a banquet; exhumations suggest more prosaic kidney failure, and, despite juicy rumours, no mercury poisoning.$dyk$, 50.0879, 14.4211, 50, $dyk$1365$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$prague-astronomical-clock$dyk$, $dyk$The Astronomical Clock$dyk$, $dyk$Six hundred years of mechanical theatre$dyk$, $dyk$Every hour on the hour, a crowd gathers beneath the Old Town Hall tower for a show that has been running since the Middle Ages. The Prague Orloj, installed in 1410, is the world's oldest astronomical clock still operating – a machine of dazzling ambition that displays not merely the time but the positions of sun and moon, the zodiac, and old Bohemian time counted from sunset. On the hour, Death – a skeleton – rings his bell and turns his hourglass, the twelve apostles parade past two windows, a Turk shakes his head, Vanity admires his mirror, and a golden rooster crows to end the performance. The clock's most famous legend is suitably dark: the councillors of Prague, so the story goes, had the clockmaker Master Hanuš blinded with a hot iron so he could never build a finer clock for a rival city – and the blind master, in revenge, climbed the tower and thrust his hand into the mechanism, stopping his masterpiece for a century and dying in the act. Historians note the real builders were Mikuláš of Kadaň and the scholar Jan Šindel, unblinded; the legend, recorded centuries later, has proven far more durable than fact.$dyk$, 50.087, 14.4207, 50, $dyk$1410$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$jan-hus-memorial$dyk$, $dyk$The Jan Hus Memorial$dyk$, $dyk$The preacher who defied an empire$dyk$, $dyk$The brooding bronze colossus dominating the Old Town Square honours the most consequential Czech who ever lived: Jan Hus, the reformist preacher burned at the stake in 1415 – a full century before Luther – for insisting that the Church answer to scripture and conscience rather than gold and power. Hus preached in Czech, not Latin, to packed congregations; he attacked the sale of indulgences and the corruption of the clergy, and even reformed Czech spelling, giving the language the accent marks it carries today. Summoned to the Council of Constance under an imperial promise of safe conduct, he was arrested on arrival, tried, and burned – the safe conduct dismissed on the principle that promises to heretics need not be kept. His death ignited the Hussite Wars, in which peasant armies wielding modified farm tools and war wagons repeatedly crushed crusader knights sent against them, and it made Hus the eternal symbol of Czech conscience against foreign power. The monument was unveiled in 1915, on the 500th anniversary of the burning, while Prague still lay under Habsburg rule – laying flowers became an act of quiet defiance, a role the statue reprised under later occupations.$dyk$, 50.088, 14.4208, 40, $dyk$1915$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$petrin-hill$dyk$, $dyk$Petřín Hill$dyk$, $dyk$Orchards, views and a little Eiffel$dyk$, $dyk$Rising green above the Lesser Town, Petřín is Prague's hill of romance – a tangle of orchards, rose gardens and winding paths where the city goes to fall in love. The tradition is official: Czech couples make pilgrimage on the first of May to kiss beneath the blossoming cherry trees at the statue of Karel Hynek Mácha, the Romantic poet whose poem 'May' every Czech can quote, guaranteeing – the custom insists – love for the year ahead. The hill's slopes fed medieval Prague with vineyards and its buildings with stone; the quarries' scars became gardens, and the old city wall crossing the hill, the 'Hunger Wall', was commissioned by Charles IV in the 1360s – legend says less for defence than as a medieval jobs programme to feed the poor, which is roughly what the chronicles suggest. At the summit stands Prague's most charming act of plagiarism: the Petřín Lookout Tower, a 63.5-metre steel homage to the Eiffel Tower, built in 1891 in mere months after Czech tourists saw the Paris original and came home inflamed. Thanks to the hill beneath it, its top actually stands higher above sea level than Eiffel's. A funicular has creaked up the slope since the same year; beside the tower waits a mirror maze in a mock castle, delighting children since 1891.$dyk$, 50.0836, 14.395, 80, $dyk$1891$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$lennon-wall$dyk$, $dyk$The Lennon Wall$dyk$, $dyk$Graffiti that fought a regime$dyk$, $dyk$The riot of colour covering this wall opposite the French Embassy began with a murder. When John Lennon was shot in December 1980, Prague's young people – locked behind the Iron Curtain, where Western pop was officially decadent – turned this quiet wall into a spontaneous shrine, painting his portrait, lyrics and messages of peace. The communist police whitewashed it. The paintings returned overnight. They whitewashed again; the wall bloomed again, now with anti-regime slogans among the doves and Beatles quotes, and the cycle became a quiet duel between paint rollers and paintbrushes that the regime, humiliatingly, could not win. Young Czechs who gathered here were beaten and arrested; the police files called them 'Lennonists', and the word became a badge of honour. After the Velvet Revolution of 1989 – whose gentle, music-soaked spirit the wall had rehearsed for a decade – the wall remained a living canvas, repainted daily by visitors from everywhere. It has been controversially whitewashed since, once by art students in 2014 leaving only 'Wall Is Over' in Lennon's font, and later restored with curated murals after vandalism – each erasure simply starting the next layer. The Knights of Malta own the wall and have made peace with immortality by graffiti. Bring a marker; adding your line is the point.$dyk$, 50.0861, 14.4069, 40, $dyk$1980$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$u-fleku-brewery$dyk$, $dyk$U Fleků$dyk$, $dyk$Five centuries of dark beer$dyk$, $dyk$Since 1499, through plagues, wars, empires, occupations and revolutions, one thing in Prague has never stopped: the brewing of dark beer at U Fleků. This labyrinthine tavern-brewery in the New Town is among the oldest continuously operating breweries in the world, and it does exactly one thing – a single beer, the famous Flekovský tmavý ležák, a black 13-degree lager with coffee-caramel depths, brewed on the premises and served nowhere else on Earth. You cannot buy it bottled; you drink it here or not at all, a marketing strategy five centuries ahead of its time. The vast halls – eight rooms seating some 1,200 drinkers under vaulted ceilings and dark wood – have hosted everyone from medieval maltsters to Czech literary bohemia; the composer and the poet argued here, the accordionist has apparently always been playing, and the waiters' legendary trick of replacing your empty mug with a full one unbidden has separated tourists from their crowns since Habsburg days (a firm 'ne, děkuji' works). The obligatory companion is Becherovka, the herbal bitter that materialises in small glasses with similar spontaneity. Yes, it's touristy; it was probably touristy in 1600. Some institutions transcend the complaint. The in-house brewing museum shows the old coppers, and the courtyard garden serves the same black beer under the sky.$dyk$, 50.0785, 14.4194, 40, $dyk$1499$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$kafka-rotating-head$dyk$, $dyk$The Rotating Head of Kafka$dyk$, $dyk$A mirror-faced monument in motion$dyk$, $dyk$Behind a shopping centre near the National Theatre, a giant silver head endlessly assembles and dismantles itself. David Černý's 'K' – eleven metres and thirty-nine tonnes of mirrored stainless steel, in forty-two independently rotating layers – portrays Franz Kafka, Prague's most famous literary son, in permanent, mesmerising identity crisis. The layers glide, the face fragments, aligns for a moment into Kafka's unmistakable gaze, then shears apart again: bureaucratic alienation as kinetic sculpture, and arguably the most fitting literary monument ever built. Kafka, who spent his short life as an insurance clerk in this city writing nightmares of faceless authority, would have – well, he would probably have been mortified, which is also fitting. Černý is Prague's licensed provocateur: his other works around town include babies with barcode faces crawling up the TV tower, two mechanical men urinating into a Czech-shaped pool while spelling quotations, and St Wenceslas riding an upside-down dead horse – a parody hanging pointedly in a passage off Wenceslas Square. The Kafka head's engineering is genuinely serious: tonnes of steel choreographed to the millimetre, with the movement patterns programmed and occasionally updated. Locals treat it as a clock of sorts: the head performs its full choreography on the hour, and the moment of alignment – the brief, complete face – is the photograph everyone waits for.$dyk$, 50.0832, 14.4218, 50, $dyk$2014$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$dancing-house$dyk$, $dyk$The Dancing House$dyk$, $dyk$Fred and Ginger on the riverbank$dyk$, $dyk$On a riverside corner among stately 19th-century facades, two glass-and-concrete towers appear to be caught mid-waltz: one pinched at the waist and flaring like a dancer's dress, leaning into its rigid partner. The Dancing House, completed in 1996 by Frank Gehry and the Croatian-Czech architect Vlado Milunić, was instantly nicknamed 'Fred and Ginger' after Fred Astaire and Ginger Rogers – the swaying glass tower is Ginger, her skirt mid-spin, held by the upright Fred with his metal dome of hair. The site's history gives the playfulness weight: the previous building was destroyed by an American bomb in February 1945, dropped in a raid that hit Prague – by most accounts a navigational error, crews believing they were over Dresden – and the plot stood empty for half a century. Its neighbour, remarkably, was the childhood home of Václav Havel, the dissident playwright turned president, who championed the project as a symbol that post-communist Prague could dare something new. Czech reaction was furious at first – critics called it a crushed can of cola defacing the historic skyline – but the city fell for its dancers in time, and the building now stars on postcards beside the castle it once allegedly insulted. There's a gallery inside, and the rooftop bar rewards visitors with a river panorama.$dyk$, 50.0755, 14.4142, 50, $dyk$1996$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$narrowest-street-certovka$dyk$, $dyk$Prague's Narrowest Street$dyk$, $dyk$A staircase with its own traffic light$dyk$, $dyk$In the Lesser Town, near the Charles Bridge, a gap between two houses poses a serious civil engineering question: what do you do when a public street is 70 centimetres wide – narrower than an opened umbrella – and pedestrians keep meeting in the middle? Prague's answer is the city's most photographed piece of bureaucratic wit: a full pair of traffic lights, one at each end, with proper red and green pedestrian signals. Push the button, wait for green, and descend the stairway single file; anyone ignoring the red risks the awkward mid-alley standoff the lights were installed to prevent – reportedly after tourists became genuinely stuck, a claim locals repeat with suspicious delight. The alley, a stepped passage leading down towards the Čertovka millstream – the 'Devil's Stream', named, legend says, after a sharp-tongued woman who lived nearby and was called a devil by her neighbours – is technically a fire gap between houses, of the kind medieval Prague left to stop flames leaping from roof to roof. Whether the lights began as safety measure or as marketing for the restaurant at the bottom is a matter of cheerful local dispute; both explanations are told as gospel.$dyk$, 50.0892, 14.4092, 40, $dyk$$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$golem-old-new-synagogue$dyk$, $dyk$The Old-New Synagogue$dyk$, $dyk$Where the Golem sleeps$dyk$, $dyk$Europe's oldest active synagogue, completed around 1270, hides Prague's greatest legend under its roof – literally, the story insists, in the attic. In the late 16th century, the tale runs, Rabbi Judah Loew ben Bezalel – a real and towering scholar of Rudolf II's Prague – moulded a man of clay from the Vltava's banks and brought him to life with a sacred word, a 'shem', placed in his mouth or inscribed on his forehead. The Golem patrolled the ghetto, protecting Prague's Jews from the pogroms and false accusations that stalked them. But the creature grew erratic – in the most famous version, the rabbi forgot to remove the shem one Sabbath eve, and the Golem ran amok until Loew tore the word from him mid-rampage. The lifeless clay was carried up to this synagogue's attic, where it lies waiting, the legend promises, for the day the community needs it again. The attic has fed the myth beautifully: it was long forbidden to enter, the exterior ladder cut short, and when researchers finally examined it, they found – nothing, which settled nothing. A Nazi agent who climbed up during the occupation is said to have died mysteriously; the SS, another story claims, dared not disturb it.$dyk$, 50.0901, 14.4183, 50, $dyk$1270$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$faust-house$dyk$, $dyk$The Faust House$dyk$, $dyk$The house with a hole to hell$dyk$, $dyk$The pink baroque mansion on Charles Square carries the most diabolical address in Prague. For centuries the Faust House has attracted stories the way its chimneys attract lightning – because an improbable series of its residents really did practise the dark-adjacent arts. In the 14th century it belonged to Prince Wenceslas of Opava, an alchemy enthusiast; in Rudolf II's day it housed Edward Kelley, the earless English alchemist (he lost the ears to a forger's punishment) who promised the emperor gold and died in prison failing; later came the obsessive chemist Ferdinand Mladota, whose experiments filled the house with explosions, mechanical tricks and moving figures that terrified visitors. Small wonder Prague concluded that Doctor Faust himself had lived here – and that when his contract with Mephistopheles expired, the Devil seized him and dragged him bodily through the ceiling, leaving a scorched hole that, the legend swears, no mason could ever brick up: each morning the new masonry lay crumbled on the floor. A poor student who later lodged in the room found coins appearing daily in a bowl – the Devil's retainer – and accepted them, until he too vanished through the ceiling. The house now belongs, deliciously, to a university medical faculty; a pharmacy operates on the ground floor, dispensing remedies on the site of damnation.$dyk$, 50.0752, 14.4188, 40, $dyk$14th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Prague$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$vysehrad-libuse$dyk$, $dyk$Vyšehrad$dyk$, $dyk$The rock where Prague was prophesied$dyk$, $dyk$On this fortress rock above the Vltava, Czech mythology places its founding moment. Here, the legend tells, ruled Princess Libuše, wisest of the three daughters of the judge Krok – a seeress who settled disputes until insulted litigants grumbled at being judged by a woman. Libuše answered by choosing a husband on her own terms: she sent envoys to find a ploughman named Přemysl, guided by her vision, and he founded the dynasty that would rule Bohemia for five centuries. Then, standing on this cliff, she fell into trance and spoke the prophecy every Czech child learns: 'I see a great city whose glory will touch the stars.' She directed her people to a spot in the forest where a man was carving a threshold – 'práh' in Czech – and commanded that the city be named Prague, 'Praha', for it. The real Vyšehrad was a royal seat by the 10th century, later ringed with baroque fortifications, and its quiet lanes now hold Prague's most extraordinary address book: the Slavín cemetery, resting place of the nation's greats, including the composers Smetana and Dvořák – Smetana, fittingly, wrote 'Vyšehrad', the swelling opening of his cycle Má vlast, about this very rock.$dyk$, 50.0642, 14.4179, 80, $dyk$10th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$belem-tower$dyk$, $dyk$Belém Tower$dyk$, $dyk$The fortress that waved the ships goodbye$dyk$, $dyk$Rising from the Tagus like a chess piece carved of lace, the Torre de Belém is Portugal's beloved icon – the last sight of home for the sailors who set out to map the world. Completed in 1519 under King Manuel I, the tower guarded the river approach to Lisbon during the Age of Discoveries, when this shoreline was the launchpad of empire: Vasco da Gama had sailed for India from Belém in 1497, and fleets bound for Brazil, Africa and Asia followed. The tower's style bears the king's name – Manueline – a uniquely Portuguese Gothic laced with maritime fantasy: carved ropes wrap the walls, stone knots, armillary spheres and crosses of the Order of Christ crowd every surface. On the northwestern turret hides the building's most charming detail: a carved rhinoceros head, Europe's first stone portrait of the beast, commemorating the live rhino the king received from India in 1515 – the same animal Dürer drew, sight unseen, in his famous print. The king later shipped the rhino to the Pope; it drowned in a shipwreck en route, arguably the unluckiest diplomatic gift in history. The tower later served as a prison, its lower chambers flooding with the tide – prisoners of successive regimes could measure time by the water at their ankles.$dyk$, 38.69155, -9.21597, 60, $dyk$1519$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$jeronimos-monastery$dyk$, $dyk$Jerónimos Monastery$dyk$, $dyk$A monument paid for in pepper$dyk$, $dyk$The vast monastery stretching along Belém's waterfront was funded by the most profitable seasoning in history. King Manuel I began it in 1501, flush with the wealth of Vasco da Gama's new sea route to India, and paid for the decades of construction with the 'pepper penny' – a tax on the spices, gems and gold flooding in from the East. It was built beside the harbour chapel where da Gama and his crew prayed through their final night ashore before sailing into the unknown in 1497; the explorer now lies entombed just inside the church door, opposite the tomb of Luís de Camões, the one-eyed soldier-poet whose epic 'The Lusiads' turned that voyage into Portugal's national myth. The church interior is a stone forest – slender trunk-like columns carved with sea monsters and coral bursting into a webbed vault so daring that when the 1755 earthquake levelled central Lisbon, this ceiling held. The cloister may be the most beautiful in Europe: two storeys of honey-coloured arcades where every column drips with carved ropes, sea creatures and armillary spheres. The monks of St Jerome prayed here for sailors' souls for three centuries – and, tradition happily adds, perfected the little custard tarts sold next door when the monastery closed in 1834.$dyk$, 38.6979, -9.2066, 80, $dyk$1501$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sao-jorge-castle$dyk$, $dyk$São Jorge Castle$dyk$, $dyk$The citadel above the city$dyk$, $dyk$Every city should be surveyed from its castle, and Lisbon insists on it: the crenellated walls of São Jorge crown the highest hill of the old town, with all seven hills, the patchwork of red roofs and the great blue sweep of the Tagus at your feet. People have fortified this summit for well over two thousand years – Phoenicians and Romans left traces, but the walls you walk are essentially Moorish, from the 11th century, when Lisbon was al-Ushbuna, a thriving port of the Islamic world. The pivotal drama came in 1147, when Portugal's first king, Afonso Henriques, besieged the city for months with the help of a rough coalition of Crusaders – many of them English, German and Flemish adventurers diverted en route to the Holy Land, whose chroniclers frankly recorded the looting that followed the surrender. Legend gives the breakthrough to the knight Martim Moniz, who spotted a gate ajar and wedged his own body into the gap, dying to hold it open for his comrades – a sacrifice that named a square, a metro station, and a permanent lesson in commitment. As a royal palace, the castle hosted the era's greatest reception: da Gama returned from India was received here. Today its pines shade peacocks, and the ramparts serve Lisbon's definitive sunset.$dyk$, 38.7139, -9.1334, 80, $dyk$11th century$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$se-cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$Lisbon Cathedral$dyk$, $dyk$The fortress-church of the Reconquista$dyk$, $dyk$With its twin battlemented towers and arrow-slit windows, Lisbon's cathedral looks less like a church than a castle wearing a rose window – and that is exactly what it was. The Sé was begun in 1147, the very year King Afonso Henriques and his Crusader allies took the city from the Moors, and it rose – pointedly – on the site of the main mosque, a statement of conquest in stone. War was still a live possibility, so the church was built to double as a strongpoint, which proved its salvation across nine centuries of Lisbon's disasters: earthquakes in the 14th century and the catastrophe of 1755 damaged chapels and cloisters, but the fortress nave stood. The building is thus Lisbon's biography in cross-section: a Romanesque body, a Gothic ambulatory, baroque patches, and in the cloister, excavations that drop through the floor of time – Moorish walls, Roman streets, Phoenician traces, layer under layer. The font where the city's favourite son, Saint Anthony of Padua, was baptised in 1195 is preserved inside; he was born a few steps away, and Lisbon has never quite forgiven Padua for claiming him.$dyk$, 38.7098, -9.1326, 50, $dyk$1147$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$monument-to-the-discoveries$dyk$, $dyk$Monument to the Discoveries$dyk$, $dyk$The stone caravel of explorers$dyk$, $dyk$A great stone prow thrusts over the Tagus, crowded with giants: the Padrão dos Descobrimentos portrays the cast of Portugal's Age of Discoveries as a 52-metre caravel forever leaving harbour. At the bow stands Henry the Navigator, the 15th-century prince who never sailed further than Morocco yet masterminded it all – funding expeditions, hoarding cartographers and pilots, and pushing his captains down the African coast past the point where maps dissolved into sea monsters. Behind him queue thirty-two figures of the epic: Vasco da Gama, who reached India; Magellan, whose expedition first circled the globe; Cabral, who claimed Brazil; the poet Camões clutching his epic; the painter Nuno Gonçalves; missionaries, kings and a lone kneeling queen, Philippa of Lancaster, Henry's English mother. The monument's own history is knottier than its stone rigging: first built in temporary materials for a 1940 exhibition staged by the Salazar dictatorship, it was rebuilt permanently in 1960 for the 500th anniversary of Henry's death, and modern Portugal still argues about a monument that celebrates the voyages while omitting their darker cargo – the empire and slave trade that followed. The plaza before it holds a giant marble wind rose and world map, a gift from South Africa, charting the routes; the rooftop viewpoint frames Belém's monuments in one sweep.$dyk$, 38.6936, -9.2056, 60, $dyk$1960$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pessoa-statue-a-brasileira$dyk$, $dyk$Fernando Pessoa at A Brasileira$dyk$, $dyk$Coffee with the poet of many selves$dyk$, $dyk$At a bronze café table outside A Brasileira, Lisbon's most famous literary ghost keeps his eternal reservation. Fernando Pessoa – hat, glasses, bow tie, one leg crossed – was a real regular at this Art Deco café in the 1920s and 30s, drinking absinthe and coffee while producing one of the strangest bodies of work in world literature. Pessoa didn't just write under pen names; he invented entire alternative poets – he called them 'heteronyms' – each with his own biography, philosophy, politics and literary style: the pagan shepherd-poet Alberto Caeiro, the classicist doctor Ricardo Reis, the futurist naval engineer Álvaro de Campos, and dozens more, over seventy at last scholarly count, who reviewed and argued with one another in print. By day he translated business letters; by night the population inside him wrote. He died in 1935, aged 47, nearly unknown, leaving a trunk stuffed with over 25,000 manuscript pages that scholars are still excavating and publishing; his masterpiece 'The Book of Disquiet' appeared only in 1982, almost half a century after his death. The statue arrived in 1988 for his centenary, and sitting in the empty chair beside him is Lisbon's most performed photo ritual. He would have found that simultaneously absurd and fitting – probably in two different poets' voices.$dyk$, 38.7108, -9.1421, 40, $dyk$1988$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$miradouro-senhora-do-monte$dyk$, $dyk$Miradouro da Senhora do Monte$dyk$, $dyk$Lisbon's highest balcony$dyk$, $dyk$Lisbon is a city of miradouros – viewpoints – and this is its crown: the highest, the calmest, and the one the locals would prefer the guidebooks kept quiet. From the pine-shaded terrace beside the little chapel of Our Lady of the Mount, the whole amphitheatre of Lisbon unfolds below you: the castle on its facing hill, the tumble of Alfama and Mouraria, the downtown grid rebuilt after the earthquake, the river opening towards the Atlantic, and the great red arc of the 25 de Abril bridge – a sibling of San Francisco's Golden Gate, built by the same American steelworks tradition and glowing the same sunset orange. The chapel behind you carries one of the city's oldest devotions: its stone chair, tradition says, once seated Saint Gens, a bishop-martyr of Roman times, and for centuries pregnant women climbed the hill to sit or pray here for a safe delivery – queens of Portugal among them. The terrace's rhythm is the point: quiet mornings with joggers and pigeons, painters at midday, and at dusk a gentle international congregation with guitars, wine and inadequate cameras, watching the light do what Lisbon light does. Bring a bottle, arrive an hour before sunset, and claim the western railing – the pines frame the castle exactly there.$dyk$, 38.7188, -9.1323, 50, $dyk$$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$azulejo-museum$dyk$, $dyk$The National Tile Museum$dyk$, $dyk$Portugal painted in blue and white$dyk$, $dyk$Portugal tells its story on ceramic squares, and this museum – set in a golden convent founded in 1509 – is where the whole glazed epic lives. The azulejo, from the Arabic 'al-zulayj', polished stone, arrived with Moorish geometry, and the Portuguese made it a national language: five centuries of tiles cover walls here, from Islamic-patterned panels through Renaissance mythologies, Dutch-influenced blue-and-white Bible scenes, and industrial Art Deco, to contemporary works – including designs from Lisbon's own metro stations, which still commission major tile art today. The collection's undisputed treasure fills an upper gallery: the Great View of Lisbon, a 23-metre panorama in blue and white assembled from around 1,300 tiles in about 1700, lovingly documenting the city's entire riverfront palace by palace, church by church – half a century before the 1755 earthquake erased most of what it shows. It is the most detailed portrait of pre-quake Lisbon in existence, a city's photograph taken before photography. The building competes with its contents: the church of the Madre de Deus convent is a gilt-and-tile explosion of baroque excess, its walls layered with golden woodwork and Delft-blue panels. The café spills into a tiled courtyard, and its walls – naturally – are decorated with tiles depicting the food.$dyk$, 38.725, -9.1136, 60, $dyk$1509$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$time-out-market$dyk$, $dyk$Time Out Market$dyk$, $dyk$The city's kitchens under one roof$dyk$, $dyk$The iron-framed market hall by Cais do Sodré has fed Lisbon since 1892 – but in 2014 it became the site of a global experiment: the world's first editorially curated food hall. The idea, hatched by the local Time Out magazine team, was simple and radical – the city's critics would pick the best of Lisbon's food, from legendary chefs to hole-in-the-wall heroes, and gather them under one roof, applying journalism's 'if it's good, it goes in' to lunch. Around forty kitchens and bars now line the vast hall: Michelin-starred names like Henrique Sá Pesseia and Marlene Vieira serve tasting plates across the counter from the city's most famous custard tarts, seafood institutions, the celebrated steak sandwich of Café de São Bento, and croquettes, cheeses and wines from every Portuguese region. You eat at long communal tables in the happy roar of a few thousand fellow diners – democratic, chaotic, delicious. The experiment worked so well it became Lisbon's most visited attraction by some counts, and the format has been exported to Miami, New York, Chicago, Dubai and beyond: a Lisbon idea now feeding the world. The original market survives alongside – flowers, fish and vegetables still trade in the morning on the far side, as they have for 130 years.$dyk$, 38.707, -9.146, 60, $dyk$2014$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$santa-justa-lift$dyk$, $dyk$The Santa Justa Lift$dyk$, $dyk$A cathedral of cast iron$dyk$, $dyk$In most cities, getting from one street to another doesn't involve a neo-Gothic iron tower. But Lisbon's hills demanded creative public transport, and in 1902 the city inaugurated this 45-metre vertical street: the Elevador de Santa Justa, connecting the downtown Baixa with the Carmo square above, sparing citizens one of the crueller climbs. Its architect, Raoul Mesnier du Ponsard, was a Portuguese engineer of French descent whose iron filigree so resembles the era's Parisian work that legend stubbornly makes him a student of Gustave Eiffel – a connection historians can't confirm but tourists refuse to surrender. The lift originally ran on steam; electric motors arrived in 1907 and the same machinery, lovingly maintained, still hauls the two polished wooden cabins up and down. Each cabin, panelled in brass and wood, carries about twenty passengers at a time – and, at busy hours, a queue below that can outlast the ride several times over (locals whisper: the identical view awaits via the free walk up through Carmo). At the top, a spiral staircase corkscrews to a viewing platform with the Baixa grid, the castle and the river arranged below like a model city. It is the only remaining vertical street lift in Lisbon – its funicular cousins climb the other hills – and a working museum piece: transport as jewellery.$dyk$, 38.7122, -9.1393, 40, $dyk$1902$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$pasteis-de-belem$dyk$, $dyk$Pastéis de Belém$dyk$, $dyk$The custard tart's secret vault$dyk$, $dyk$Behind this blue awning, guarded better than some state secrets, lives the original recipe of Portugal's greatest gift to the world's coffee breaks. When Portugal dissolved its monasteries in 1834, the monks of the neighbouring Jerónimos Monastery – who had long baked little custard tarts, reputedly using yolks left over because egg whites went to starch clerical robes and clarify wine – sold their secret to the family of a local sugar refinery. In 1837 the shop began selling 'Pastéis de Belém', and it has never stopped: the same family business, the same tiled warren of rooms, and the same recipe, known in full – the staff insist – to only a handful of people at a time, who mix the base in a locked room called the Oficina do Segredo, the Secret Workshop, and are said never to travel together. Only tarts baked here may legally use the name Pastéis de Belém; everywhere else in the world they are pastéis de nata, and the distinction is enforced with courteous iron. The production line runs to tens of thousands of tarts a day, served warm, blistered brown on top, with cinnamon and icing sugar on the side.$dyk$, 38.6975, -9.2032, 40, $dyk$1837$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tram-28-martim-moniz$dyk$, $dyk$Tram 28 at Martim Moniz$dyk$, $dyk$The rollercoaster with timetables$dyk$, $dyk$The little yellow tram waiting at Martim Moniz square is about to perform a small daily miracle. Route 28 threads the impossible: through Graça, Alfama, the Baixa and up to Estrela, its wooden 'Remodelado' cars – built in the 1930s and lovingly rebuilt ever since – squeeze through medieval lanes where the track's clearance from house walls can be measured in hand-widths, climb gradients that would embarrass some funiculars, and take corners so tight the wheels shriek like opera. Lisbon keeps these vintage cars on the hill routes not for nostalgia but necessity: modern trams are simply too long, too wide and too rigid for the old town's geometry – the antiques are the only machines for the job. Inside it's all polished wood benches, brass fittings, rattling windows and a driver working a handbrake crank like a ship's engineer. The full route takes about 50 minutes when traffic and double-parked vans permit – the classic advice is to board here at the northern terminus for a seat, guard your pockets (the 28 is as famous among pickpockets as among photographers), and surrender to the ride: the city's greatest-hits tour for the price of a transit ticket, soundtracked by steel on steel and the occasional exchange of opinions between driver and delivery van.$dyk$, 38.716, -9.1359, 50, $dyk$1930s$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$ravens-of-st-vincent$dyk$, $dyk$São Vicente and the Ravens$dyk$, $dyk$The saint who sailed with ravens$dyk$, $dyk$Look at any Lisbon lamppost, manhole cover or city flag and you'll find the same strange cargo: a ship flanked by two black ravens. The white monastery church crowning this hill – São Vicente de Fora – holds the story. Vincent was a Spanish deacon martyred by the Romans in 304; after his death, the legend says, ravens guarded his body from scavenging beasts. Centuries later, his relics rested at a cape in the far south of Portugal – still called Cabo de São Vicente – watched over, pilgrims reported, by a perpetual guard of ravens. In 1173, Portugal's first king, Afonso Henriques, fresh from taking Lisbon, ordered the saint's remains brought to his new city by sea. Two ravens, the chronicle-legend insists, escorted the boat the whole way – one at the prow, one at the stern – and the image sailed straight onto Lisbon's coat of arms, where it remains eight and a half centuries later. Descendants of the escort ravens, tradition claimed, lived in the cathedral cloister for generations; the last cloister ravens died out only in recent decades, a loss Lisbon still mildly mourns.$dyk$, 38.7148, -9.1275, 60, $dyk$1173$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$sao-domingos-church$dyk$, $dyk$Igreja de São Domingos$dyk$, $dyk$The church that refused to die$dyk$, $dyk$Step inside this church and you enter Lisbon's rawest room: scorched pillars, cracked stone, walls the colour of embers – a building that has kept its wounds on display. São Domingos has absorbed everything history could throw: founded in the 13th century, it hosted royal weddings and the tribunals of the Inquisition, whose sentences were read here and carried out in the square outside. Its darkest hour came in April 1506, when a mob, whipped up during plague and famine after an incident at mass, launched three days of massacre against Lisbon's 'New Christians' – Jews forcibly converted a decade earlier; as many as two thousand people were burned and killed in the surrounding streets. A memorial in the square now marks the atrocity, dedicated in 2008, five centuries late. The building itself has died twice: the 1755 earthquake brought down its vaults during All Saints' mass, and in 1959 a fire gutted the interior so thoroughly that restoration in the 1990s chose honesty over cosmetics – the interior was stabilised, not prettified, leaving the fire-eaten pillars bare witness.$dyk$, 38.714, -9.1389, 40, $dyk$1506$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Lisbon$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$severa-mouraria-fado$dyk$, $dyk$Rua do Capelão, Mouraria$dyk$, $dyk$The alley where fado was born$dyk$, $dyk$This narrow lane in Mouraria – the old Moorish quarter, kept outside the walls after the Reconquista – is the mythic birthplace of fado, the aching song of longing that became Portugal's soul in music. And the myth has a face: Maria Severa Onofriana, a tavern-keeper's daughter born in 1820, tall, black-shawled, guitar in hand, who sang in the taverns of this street and died of tuberculosis at just 26. Severa was fado's first star and its founding legend: her scandalous love affair with the Count of Vimioso, a bullfighting aristocrat who carried her into salons the poor quarter's singer was never meant to enter, gave fado its eternal theme – doomed love across impossible distance – and its first celebrity gossip. Nothing she sang was recorded; no photograph exists; and precisely this vacuum made her immortal, filled by plays, Portugal's first sound film ('A Severa', 1931), novels and a hundred fados about her. Fadistas still sing of Severa, and the black shawl worn by female fado singers is her uniform, mourning her by dress code a century and a half on. The lane wears its history: a mural of the guitar, tiled portraits of Mouraria's fado figures, and at the corner the little chapel that named the street.$dyk$, 38.7155, -9.1355, 40, $dyk$1840s$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$acropolis-parthenon$dyk$, $dyk$The Parthenon, Acropolis$dyk$, $dyk$The temple that defined the West$dyk$, $dyk$Crowning the sacred rock above Athens stands the building against which Western architecture has measured itself for 2,500 years. The Parthenon rose between 447 and 432 BC, at the summit of Athens' golden age under Pericles, as a temple to Athena Parthenos – the Virgin Athena – whose colossal statue by Pheidias, twelve metres of gold and ivory, once glowed in its inner chamber. The marble came from Mount Pentelicus; the perfection came from calculated imperfection. The temple contains scarcely a straight line: the platform bows gently upward, the columns lean inward and swell at mid-height, corner columns are subtly thicker – dozens of optical refinements that trick the eye into seeing flawless straightness where rigid geometry would have looked lifeless and sagging. It has been a temple, a church of the Virgin Mary, a mosque – and a ruin since a single evening in 1687, when a Venetian mortar bomb hit the gunpowder the occupying Ottomans had stored inside, blowing out the building's heart. In the early 1800s Lord Elgin's agents stripped much of the surviving sculpture, now in the British Museum – a wound Greece still actively campaigns to heal. Restoration has run for decades, craftsmen carving new Pentelic marble by hand; the crane is by now almost a classical feature.$dyk$, 37.97153, 23.7261, 60, $dyk$447 BC$dyk$, true, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 0)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$erechtheion-caryatids$dyk$, $dyk$The Caryatids, Erechtheion$dyk$, $dyk$The maidens who carry a temple$dyk$, $dyk$Six marble maidens stand on the Acropolis bearing a temple roof on their heads – and they have been standing there, in graceful, impossible patience, since around 415 BC. The Caryatids form the porch of the Erechtheion, the Acropolis's other great temple, holier in some ways than the Parthenon itself: this irregular, split-level building gathered the rock's oldest cults, sheltering the ancient olive-wood statue of Athena, the mark where Poseidon's trident struck, and the tomb of the mythical king Erechtheus. The maidens are engineering disguised as elegance – their load-bearing necks are strengthened by the sculpted hair falling in thick braids down their backs, structure hidden in coiffure. Each stands differently, weight on alternating legs, garments falling like fluted columns. The figures you see outside are casts: five originals stand in climate-controlled dignity in the Acropolis Museum below the hill, where restorers cleaned them with lasers, live before visitors. The sixth is in the British Museum, taken by Lord Elgin – and here the legend enters: Athenians long told that on quiet nights the five remaining sisters could be heard weeping, calling for the stolen sixth. In the museum, her empty place is left conspicuously vacant among the five – a marble absence, waiting.$dyk$, 37.9721, 23.7266, 40, $dyk$421 BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$work_of_art$dyk$, 1)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$temple-of-hephaestus$dyk$, $dyk$Temple of Hephaestus$dyk$, $dyk$The best-preserved Greek temple$dyk$, $dyk$While the Parthenon exists in glorious fragments, its little contemporary on the hill across the Agora survives almost whole: the Temple of Hephaestus is the best-preserved ancient Greek temple in existence, its full colonnade and roof-line intact after nearly 2,500 years. Completed around 415 BC, it honours the lame smith-god of fire and craft together with Athena as patroness of artisans – a working-class dedication for a working district, since the surrounding quarter housed the city's metalworkers and potters, and slag from ancient foundries has been found nearby. Locals long called it the 'Theseion', reading the hero Theseus into its sculpted labours; the name stuck to the whole neighbourhood, and to the metro station, even after scholars re-identified the true tenant. The temple owes its survival to continuous employment: converted around the 7th century into the church of St George, it stayed roofed, used and repaired while its abandoned siblings were quarried. In the 19th century it served as burial place for European philhellenes who died for Greek independence, and it hosted the official welcome of the first modern Greek king in 1834. It became a museum – Greece's first, by some counting – and stands today above the Agora's green ruins, offering the classic view of what an ancient temple actually looked like, rather than what time usually leaves.$dyk$, 37.9755, 23.7213, 60, $dyk$415 BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 2)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$temple-of-olympian-zeus$dyk$, $dyk$Temple of Olympian Zeus$dyk$, $dyk$Seven centuries to build a giant$dyk$, $dyk$The colossal columns rising from this open field – seventeen metres tall, in extravagant Corinthian dress – are the remains of the longest construction project in the ancient world. The tyrant Peisistratos's family began a giant temple to Zeus here around 520 BC, dreaming of out-building every sanctuary in Greece; the tyranny fell, and the democratic Athenians, viewing such megalomania with distaste, left the stump untouched for over three centuries as a monument to hubris. A Hellenistic king restarted it in 174 BC with a Roman architect and a new Corinthian design; his money died with him. It fell to a Roman emperor – Hadrian, the great Grecophile – to finish the job around AD 131, nearly 650 years after ground-breaking, installing a giant gold-and-ivory Zeus and, the sources note, an equally giant statue of himself. Of the 104 original columns, fifteen stand; a sixteenth lies where an 1852 storm toppled it, its marble drums spread like a sliced column diagram, and it has become the ruin's most photographed feature. Beside the site stands Hadrian's Arch, which once carried a pointed double inscription – on one side 'This is Athens, the ancient city of Theseus', on the other 'This is the city of Hadrian, and not of Theseus' – antiquity's most elegant piece of urban rebranding.$dyk$, 37.9693, 23.7331, 70, $dyk$131$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 3)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$panathenaic-stadium$dyk$, $dyk$The Panathenaic Stadium$dyk$, $dyk$The all-marble home of the first modern Olympics$dyk$, $dyk$The gleaming horseshoe of white marble before you is the only stadium in the world built entirely of the stuff – and the place where the Olympic Games came back from the dead. A stadium has stood in this ravine since about 330 BC, hosting the athletic contests of the Panathenaic festival; the Roman-era magnate Herodes Atticus rebuilt it in marble around AD 144, seating perhaps fifty thousand. Centuries of quarrying reduced it to a ghostly hollow – until the Olympic revival found it. Rebuilt in Pentelic marble through the gift of the businessman George Averoff, whose statue stands at the entrance, the stadium hosted the first modern Olympic Games in April 1896. Its defining moment arrived with the marathon – a race invented for those Games to honour the legendary run of a messenger from the battlefield of Marathon. When word spread that a Greek was leading, the stadium came apart; the shepherd-turned-water-carrier Spyridon Louis entered the arena to a roar that witnesses never forgot, princes running the final stretch beside him. Louis never raced again; he didn't need to – 'egine Louis', 'to do a Louis', still means to run off swiftly in Greek.$dyk$, 37.9684, 23.741, 80, $dyk$1896$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$building$dyk$, 4)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$academy-of-athens-statues$dyk$, $dyk$The Academy of Athens$dyk$, $dyk$Plato and Socrates enthroned in marble$dyk$, $dyk$Flanking the steps of this dazzling neoclassical palace sit the two Athenians who invented Western philosophy: Socrates and Plato, enthroned in Pentelic marble, with Athena and Apollo watching from soaring columns above. The Academy of Athens, completed in 1885 as part of a grand architectural trilogy with the university and national library, is a 19th-century love letter to the golden age – built of the same Pentelic marble as the Parthenon, its sculpted pediment a classical assembly of gods, its details picked out in gold and colour as ancient temples truly were. The seated pair below make an instructive study: Socrates, who wrote nothing, sits bareheaded and plain, the eternal street-corner questioner – the man who compared himself to a gadfly stinging the lazy horse of Athens, and whom the city rewarded with a death sentence, the cup of hemlock, in 399 BC. Plato, his student, who wrote everything down and founded in the grove of Akademos the school that gave every 'academy' on Earth its name, sits scroll in hand. Between them, in a sense, sits all of it: the question and the answer, the martyr and the institution. The building houses Greece's national academy of sciences and letters – mortals now deliberating daily under the marble gaze of their unbeatable predecessors.$dyk$, 37.98, 23.733, 50, $dyk$1885$dyk$, false, $dyk$history$dyk$, $dyk$historical_figure$dyk$, 5)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$national-garden$dyk$, $dyk$The National Garden$dyk$, $dyk$A queen's jungle in the city centre$dyk$, $dyk$Slip through a gate beside the parliament and Athens goes suddenly green and quiet: 15.5 hectares of deep shade, winding paths, duck ponds and unexpected ruins – the National Garden, planted from 1840 as the private paradise of Queen Amalia, the German-born first queen of modern Greece. Amalia took gardening as seriously as statecraft; she reportedly spent hours daily directing the works, and had exotic species shipped from around the world – including a batch of young Washingtonia palms whose towering descendants still line the entrance avenue, arguably the most regal thing she left behind. The navy, gossip claimed, was occasionally diverted to plant transport. Her garden absorbed antiquity as it grew: paths circle Roman mosaic floors, column stumps and a Corinthian capital or two, casually fenced, as if ruins were a species of shrub. When the monarchy departed, the royal garden became national, and it now serves as the city's daily exhale – pensioners with newspapers under the century-old trees, children at the little zoo and playgrounds, cats employed in professional sunbathing, and the small café by the pond conducting its unhurried business. It is not grand; it is necessary. Athenians measure summer by the temperature difference inside these gates – reliably a few merciful degrees cooler than the streets outside.$dyk$, 37.9732, 23.7372, 80, $dyk$1840$dyk$, true, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$natural_spaces$dyk$, 6)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$acropolis-museum$dyk$, $dyk$The Acropolis Museum$dyk$, $dyk$Glass floors over a buried city$dyk$, $dyk$The luminous glass building at the foot of the sacred rock was designed as both a museum and an argument. Opened in 2009, the Acropolis Museum holds everything portable that the rock has yielded – and holds it pointedly: the top floor, the Parthenon Gallery, is rotated askew from the building's grid to align exactly with the Parthenon visible through its glass walls, and displays the temple's great frieze at true scale, original blocks alternating with white plaster casts of the pieces held in London. The gaps are the argument: Greece's standing case for reuniting the sculptures, built in concrete and glass. The collection beneath is breathtaking on its own terms – the five original Caryatids, serene on their balcony; the archaic gallery's smiling maidens, the korai, still carrying traces of their ancient paint; the Kritios Boy on the cusp of classical realism. And the museum's cleverest exhibit is underfoot: the building stands on pillars above an excavated ancient neighbourhood, and glass floors let you watch two and a half millennia of ordinary Athens – houses, baths, workshops, streets – proceeding beneath your shoes. The ruins below opened to visitors as their own circuit, making this perhaps the only museum you can walk under.$dyk$, 37.9686, 23.7286, 60, $dyk$2009$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$art$dyk$, 7)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$anafiotika-plaka$dyk$, $dyk$Anafiotika$dyk$, $dyk$A Cycladic island under the Acropolis$dyk$, $dyk$Climb the stairways above Plaka and, without warning, the city changes country: whitewashed cube houses, blue shutters, bougainvillea over doorways barely shoulder-wide, cats asleep on sun-warmed steps – a Cycladic island village clinging to the north slope of the Acropolis. Anafiotika is exactly what it looks like. In the 1840s, master builders from the small island of Anafi came to construct the new capital and its royal palace; homesick and unhoused, they did the sensible thing after their shifts: built their own neighbourhood, in the only style they knew, on the empty rock above the town – reputedly at night, exploiting an Ottoman-era custom that a house raised between sunset and sunrise, with the roof on by dawn, could not be torn down. The authorities objected, the houses stayed; islanders are patient people. Two small churches from the period, lovingly kept, anchor the quarter, and perhaps forty-odd houses survive, still inhabited, laundered and painted, their lanes too narrow for any vehicle – groceries travel the last stretch by hand. There are no shops, no tavernas, no sights in the official sense: the neighbourhood is the sight, a functioning fragment of the Aegean pasted onto the ancient rock.$dyk$, 37.9718, 23.7287, 60, $dyk$1840s$dyk$, false, $dyk$otium$dyk$, $dyk$leisure$dyk$, 8)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$evzones-syntagma$dyk$, $dyk$The Evzones, Syntagma Square$dyk$, $dyk$The slow-motion guard of the Unknown Soldier$dyk$, $dyk$In front of parliament, two soldiers perform one of the world's most extraordinary pieces of military choreography – in skirts and pom-pom shoes, and in almost total slow motion. The Evzones, an elite presidential guard tracing its lineage to 1868, keep watch over the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier around the clock. Their uniform is a walking history lesson: the foustanella kilt carries 400 pleats, one – tradition insists – for each year of Ottoman occupation; the red cap's long black tassel, the white wool leggings, and the tsarouchia – red leather clogs crowned with black pom-poms, each shoe reinforced with dozens of nails whose crash on the marble is the ceremony's percussion. The pom-poms, guides enjoy claiming, once hid blades; the truer answer – weatherproofing the toe seam – has never dented the story. Guards stand motionless for hour-long shifts, twice per day of duty; the raised-leg, suspended-animation march of the changeover exists, the guard explains, to revive circulation after the stillness without breaking dignity. Selection is competitive – height requirements, elite training, and the composure to ignore tourists attempting comedy at close range; a colleague in fatigues watches, adjusting a fold of a motionless guard's uniform like a valet to a statue.$dyk$, 37.9754, 23.7368, 50, $dyk$1868$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 9)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$syntagma-metro-museum$dyk$, $dyk$Syntagma Metro Station$dyk$, $dyk$The subway that doubles as a museum$dyk$, $dyk$Building a subway under Athens is archaeology with a timetable. When the city excavated its new metro lines in the 1990s, every shaft became a dig site: the works turned into the largest archaeological excavation in the city's history, unearthing some 50,000 artefacts – graves, roads, aqueducts, workshops, a river's ancient bed – before a single train ran. The stations, opened in 2000, honour the haul: Syntagma station is effectively a small museum you happen to catch trains in. On the concourse, glass cases display finds from the site – vases, coins, burial offerings – and one wall preserves the excavation itself as a giant cross-section behind glass: strata from prehistoric to Byzantine stacked like a layer cake of civilisations, with a grave, a road surface and clay pipes exactly where the diggers found them, labelled by century. Commuters stride past a display many national museums would envy; tourists photograph the wall while missing their train. Other stations continue the idea – Acropolis station shows casts of the Parthenon frieze, Monastiraki exposes the ancient riverbed of the Eridanos under a glass floor – and the metro's diggers still supply museums: extensions keep hitting antiquity, which in Athens is less an obstacle than a certainty. The trains, it should be said, also run rather well.$dyk$, 37.9756, 23.7354, 40, $dyk$2000$dyk$, true, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 10)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$tower-of-the-winds$dyk$, $dyk$The Tower of the Winds$dyk$, $dyk$The world's first weather station$dyk$, $dyk$The elegant octagonal tower at the edge of the Roman Agora may be the most over-engineered timepiece of antiquity – an all-in-one clock, weather station and compass, built around the 1st century BC by the astronomer Andronikos of Kyrrhos. Each of its eight marble faces bears a flying relief of one of the wind gods – Boreas the bitter north, Zephyros the gentle west, and their six brothers – so citizens could read the wind's direction against the frieze, aided originally by a rotating bronze Triton on the roof serving as weathervane. Below the gods, the walls carry the engraved lines of eight sundials; and for sunless days, the interior housed a sophisticated water clock, fed by a spring on the Acropolis slope, whose channels are still traceable in the floor. In effect: time, weather and orientation, one building, no batteries, two thousand years early. The tower's survival matched its versatility – it served as a church, and in Ottoman times as a tekke of whirling dervishes, whose ceremonies under the wind gods must rank among history's finest architectural remixes. The Andronikos design echoes across centuries: it directly inspired towers and observatories from Oxford to Sevastopol, and its name in Greek – Aerides, 'the winds' – still labels the whole charming quarter around it.$dyk$, 37.9741, 23.7268, 40, $dyk$1st century BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$funfact$dyk$, null, 11)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$areopagus-hill$dyk$, $dyk$The Areopagus$dyk$, $dyk$The rock of gods on trial$dyk$, $dyk$The bare, slippery rock outcrop below the Acropolis entrance – polished to glass by millennia of feet – carries more mythology per square metre than almost any spot in Greece. This is the Areopagus, the 'Hill of Ares': named, legend says, for the war god himself, who was tried here by his fellow Olympians for killing Poseidon's son – the first murder trial in the world's imagination, with gods for both defendant and jury. Athenian drama returned the favour: in Aeschylus's Oresteia, it is on this rock that Orestes, hounded by the Furies for avenging his father by killing his mother, is tried and acquitted when Athena casts the deciding vote – mythology's founding charter for the idea that vengeance must yield to law. The real Athens matched the myths: the city's most ancient council, guardian of homicide law, met here and took its name from the rock. And one spring day around AD 51, a travelling tentmaker from Tarsus stood here and told the Athenians about an 'unknown god' they already had an altar to – the Apostle Paul's Areopagus sermon, one of history's pivotal speeches, commemorated by a bronze plaque of its Greek text at the foot of the steps.$dyk$, 37.9722, 23.724, 50, $dyk$$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 12)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$lycabettus-hill$dyk$, $dyk$Lycabettus Hill$dyk$, $dyk$The rock Athena dropped$dyk$, $dyk$The steep limestone cone rising abruptly from the city grid – higher than the Acropolis, crowned with a tiny white chapel – shouldn't really be there, and mythology agrees. The legend: Athena, building her Acropolis, was carrying an enormous rock from Mount Penteli to make her citadel taller. En route, a crow – or in other tellings two dark-omened birds – brought her the news that the daughters of King Cecrops had opened the forbidden box hiding the serpent-child Erichthonius. Startled, the goddess dropped her rock where she stood; it has been standing there ever since, 277 metres of divine fumble now called Lycabettus. The name more likely means 'hill of wolves', which prowled it well into historical times – the ancients considered it wild, outside the city's life, which is precisely why it stayed bare while Athens swallowed everything else. Athena, holding a grudge, allegedly condemned the tattling crow's species to be black forever. Today the wolf hill is the city's romantic upper balcony: a funicular tunnels up from Kolonaki, a zigzag path climbs through pines and agaves for the devout, and at the summit the whitewashed chapel of St George presides over the definitive Athens panorama – Acropolis below eye level, city to the horizon, and on clear days the island of Aegina floating in the Saronic Gulf.$dyk$, 37.9818, 23.7431, 80, $dyk$$dyk$, true, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 13)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

values ((select id from public.citypacks where city = $dyk$Athens$dyk$ limit 1), $dyk$socrates-prison$dyk$, $dyk$The 'Prison of Socrates'$dyk$, $dyk$The cave that keeps the name$dyk$, $dyk$On the pine-covered slope of Philopappos Hill, three barred openings gape in a limestone face – rooms cut into living rock, with grooves and cuttings betraying vanished doors and fittings. For generations, Athenians and their guides have called this the Prison of Socrates: the cell where the philosopher, condemned in 399 BC for 'corrupting the youth' and 'impiety', spent his final month while the sacred ship's voyage to Delos delayed all executions – the month of the great prison dialogues, where he refused escape plans, discoursed on the soul's immortality, and finally drank the hemlock among weeping friends, scolding them gently for the tears. It is a magnificent address for the story, and almost certainly wrong: archaeologists identify the actual state prison's foundations down in the Agora excavations, where small clay medicine cups – hemlock-sized – were poignantly found, and read these rock rooms as parts of an ancient house or workshop. The name, however, has proven immune to evidence for two centuries, and the cave earned real history of its own: during the Second World War, treasures of the national collections were hidden here behind a concrete wall to keep them from the occupiers – the fake prison of the man who owned nothing, guarding the nation's gold.$dyk$, 37.9683, 23.719, 40, $dyk$5th century BC$dyk$, false, $dyk$headline$dyk$, null, 14)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

